window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH["richardiii"] = {"id":"richardiii","title":"Richard III","kind":"play","source":"MIT Shakespeare (Complete Moby Shakespeare)","sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","sourceRevision":"6b82db852c7322dd33e95db347f0ddfc812c6409","sections":[{"id":"1.1","label":"Act 1 · SCENE I. London. A street.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER, solus","id":"richardiii:1.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now is the winter of our discontent\nMade glorious summer by this sun of York;\nAnd all the clouds that lour'd upon our house\nIn the deep bosom of the ocean buried.\nNow are our brows bound with victorious wreaths;\nOur bruised arms hung up for monuments;\nOur stern alarums changed to merry meetings,\nOur dreadful marches to delightful measures.\nGrim-visaged war hath smooth'd his wrinkled front;\nAnd now, instead of mounting barbed steeds\nTo fright the souls of fearful adversaries,\nHe capers nimbly in a lady's chamber\nTo the lascivious pleasing of a lute.\nBut I, that am not shaped for sportive tricks,\nNor made to court an amorous looking-glass;\nI, that am rudely stamp'd, and want love's majesty\nTo strut before a wanton ambling nymph;\nI, that am curtail'd of this fair proportion,\nCheated of feature by dissembling nature,\nDeformed, unfinish'd, sent before my time\nInto this breathing world, scarce half made up,\nAnd that so lamely and unfashionable\nThat dogs bark at me as I halt by them;\nWhy, I, in this weak piping time of peace,\nHave no delight to pass away the time,\nUnless to spy my shadow in the sun\nAnd descant on mine own deformity:\nAnd therefore, since I cannot prove a lover,\nTo entertain these fair well-spoken days,\nI am determined to prove a villain\nAnd hate the idle pleasures of these days.\nPlots have I laid, inductions dangerous,\nBy drunken prophecies, libels and dreams,\nTo set my brother Clarence and the king\nIn deadly hate the one against the other:\nAnd if King Edward be as true and just\nAs I am subtle, false and treacherous,\nThis day should Clarence closely be mew'd up,\nAbout a prophecy, which says that 'G'\nOf Edward's heirs the murderer shall be.\nDive, thoughts, down to my soul: here\nClarence comes.","sourceLine":"1.1.1","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLARENCE, guarded, and BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brother, good day; what means this armed guard\nThat waits upon your grace?","sourceLine":"1.1.43","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His majesty\nTendering my person's safety, hath appointed\nThis conduct to convey me to the Tower.","sourceLine":"1.1.45","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon what cause?","sourceLine":"1.1.48","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because my name is George.","sourceLine":"1.1.49","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alack, my lord, that fault is none of yours;\nHe should, for that, commit your godfathers:\nO, belike his majesty hath some intent\nThat you shall be new-christen'd in the Tower.\nBut what's the matter, Clarence? may I know?","sourceLine":"1.1.50","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yea, Richard, when I know; for I protest\nAs yet I do not: but, as I can learn,\nHe hearkens after prophecies and dreams;\nAnd from the cross-row plucks the letter G.\nAnd says a wizard told him that by G\nHis issue disinherited should be;\nAnd, for my name of George begins with G,\nIt follows in his thought that I am he.\nThese, as I learn, and such like toys as these\nHave moved his highness to commit me now.","sourceLine":"1.1.55","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, this it is, when men are ruled by women:\n'Tis not the king that sends you to the Tower:\nMy Lady Grey his wife, Clarence, 'tis she\nThat tempers him to this extremity.\nWas it not she and that good man of worship,\nAnthony Woodville, her brother there,\nThat made him send Lord Hastings to the Tower,\nFrom whence this present day he is deliver'd?\nWe are not safe, Clarence; we are not safe.","sourceLine":"1.1.65","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By heaven, I think there's no man is secure\nBut the queen's kindred and night-walking heralds\nThat trudge betwixt the king and Mistress Shore.\nHeard ye not what an humble suppliant\nLord hastings was to her for his delivery?","sourceLine":"1.1.74","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Humbly complaining to her deity\nGot my lord chamberlain his liberty.\nI'll tell you what; I think it is our way,\nIf we will keep in favour with the king,\nTo be her men and wear her livery:\nThe jealous o'erworn widow and herself,\nSince that our brother dubb'd them gentlewomen.\nAre mighty gossips in this monarchy.","sourceLine":"1.1.79","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I beseech your graces both to pardon me;\nHis majesty hath straitly given in charge\nThat no man shall have private conference,\nOf what degree soever, with his brother.","sourceLine":"1.1.87","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even so; an't please your worship, Brakenbury,\nYou may partake of any thing we say:\nWe speak no treason, man: we say the king\nIs wise and virtuous, and his noble queen\nWell struck in years, fair, and not jealous;\nWe say that Shore's wife hath a pretty foot,\nA cherry lip, a bonny eye, a passing pleasing tongue;\nAnd that the queen's kindred are made gentle-folks:\nHow say you sir? Can you deny all this?","sourceLine":"1.1.91","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With this, my lord, myself have nought to do.","sourceLine":"1.1.100","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Naught to do with mistress Shore! I tell thee, fellow,\nHe that doth naught with her, excepting one,\nWere best he do it secretly, alone.","sourceLine":"1.1.101","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What one, my lord?","sourceLine":"1.1.104","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her husband, knave: wouldst thou betray me?","sourceLine":"1.1.105","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I beseech your grace to pardon me, and withal\nForbear your conference with the noble duke.","sourceLine":"1.1.106","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We know thy charge, Brakenbury, and will obey.","sourceLine":"1.1.108","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We are the queen's abjects, and must obey.\nBrother, farewell: I will unto the king;\nAnd whatsoever you will employ me in,\nWere it to call King Edward's widow sister,\nI will perform it to enfranchise you.\nMeantime, this deep disgrace in brotherhood\nTouches me deeper than you can imagine.","sourceLine":"1.1.109","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know it pleaseth neither of us well.","sourceLine":"1.1.116","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, your imprisonment shall not be long;\nMeantime, have patience.","sourceLine":"1.1.117","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must perforce. Farewell.","sourceLine":"1.1.119","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.1:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt CLARENCE, BRAKENBURY, and Guard","id":"richardiii:1.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, tread the path that thou shalt ne'er return.\nSimple, plain Clarence! I do love thee so,\nThat I will shortly send thy soul to heaven,\nIf heaven will take the present at our hands.\nBut who comes here? the new-deliver'd Hastings?","sourceLine":"1.1.120","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good time of day unto my gracious lord!","sourceLine":"1.1.125","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As much unto my good lord chamberlain!\nWell are you welcome to the open air.\nHow hath your lordship brook'd imprisonment?","sourceLine":"1.1.126","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With patience, noble lord, as prisoners must:\nBut I shall live, my lord, to give them thanks\nThat were the cause of my imprisonment.","sourceLine":"1.1.129","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No doubt, no doubt; and so shall Clarence too;\nFor they that were your enemies are his,\nAnd have prevail'd as much on him as you.","sourceLine":"1.1.132","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"More pity that the eagle should be mew'd,\nWhile kites and buzzards prey at liberty.","sourceLine":"1.1.135","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What news abroad?","sourceLine":"1.1.137","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No news so bad abroad as this at home;\nThe King is sickly, weak and melancholy,\nAnd his physicians fear him mightily.","sourceLine":"1.1.138","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by Saint Paul, this news is bad indeed.\nO, he hath kept an evil diet long,\nAnd overmuch consumed his royal person:\n'Tis very grievous to be thought upon.\nWhat, is he in his bed?","sourceLine":"1.1.141","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is.","sourceLine":"1.1.146","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go you before, and I will follow you.","sourceLine":"1.1.147","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He cannot live, I hope; and must not die\nTill George be pack'd with post-horse up to heaven.\nI'll in, to urge his hatred more to Clarence,\nWith lies well steel'd with weighty arguments;\nAnd, if I fall not in my deep intent,\nClarence hath not another day to live:\nWhich done, God take King Edward to his mercy,\nAnd leave the world for me to bustle in!\nFor then I'll marry Warwick's youngest daughter.\nWhat though I kill'd her husband and her father?\nThe readiest way to make the wench amends\nIs to become her husband and her father:\nThe which will I; not all so much for love\nAs for another secret close intent,\nBy marrying her which I must reach unto.\nBut yet I run before my horse to market:\nClarence still breathes; Edward still lives and reigns:\nWhen they are gone, then must I count my gains.","sourceLine":"1.1.148","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.1:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:1.1:40"}],"scene":1},{"id":"1.2","label":"Act 1 · SCENE II. The same. Another street.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the corpse of KING HENRY the Sixth, Gentlemen with halberds to guard it; LADY ANNE being the mourner","id":"richardiii:1.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Set down, set down your honourable load,\nIf honour may be shrouded in a hearse,\nWhilst I awhile obsequiously lament\nThe untimely fall of virtuous Lancaster.\nPoor key-cold figure of a holy king!\nPale ashes of the house of Lancaster!\nThou bloodless remnant of that royal blood!\nBe it lawful that I invocate thy ghost,\nTo hear the lamentations of Poor Anne,\nWife to thy Edward, to thy slaughter'd son,\nStabb'd by the selfsame hand that made these wounds!\nLo, in these windows that let forth thy life,\nI pour the helpless balm of my poor eyes.\nCursed be the hand that made these fatal holes!\nCursed be the heart that had the heart to do it!\nCursed the blood that let this blood from hence!\nMore direful hap betide that hated wretch,\nThat makes us wretched by the death of thee,\nThan I can wish to adders, spiders, toads,\nOr any creeping venom'd thing that lives!\nIf ever he have child, abortive be it,\nProdigious, and untimely brought to light,\nWhose ugly and unnatural aspect\nMay fright the hopeful mother at the view;\nAnd that be heir to his unhappiness!\nIf ever he have wife, let her he made\nA miserable by the death of him\nAs I am made by my poor lord and thee!\nCome, now towards Chertsey with your holy load,\nTaken from Paul's to be interred there;\nAnd still, as you are weary of the weight,\nRest you, whiles I lament King Henry's corse.","sourceLine":"1.2.1","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, you that bear the corse, and set it down.","sourceLine":"1.2.33","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What black magician conjures up this fiend,\nTo stop devoted charitable deeds?","sourceLine":"1.2.34","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Villains, set down the corse; or, by Saint Paul,\nI'll make a corse of him that disobeys.","sourceLine":"1.2.36","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, stand back, and let the coffin pass.","sourceLine":"1.2.38","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"richardiii:1.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unmanner'd dog! stand thou, when I command:\nAdvance thy halbert higher than my breast,\nOr, by Saint Paul, I'll strike thee to my foot,\nAnd spurn upon thee, beggar, for thy boldness.","sourceLine":"1.2.39","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, do you tremble? are you all afraid?\nAlas, I blame you not; for you are mortal,\nAnd mortal eyes cannot endure the devil.\nAvaunt, thou dreadful minister of hell!\nThou hadst but power over his mortal body,\nHis soul thou canst not have; therefore be gone.","sourceLine":"1.2.43","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet saint, for charity, be not so curst.","sourceLine":"1.2.49","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Foul devil, for God's sake, hence, and trouble us not;\nFor thou hast made the happy earth thy hell,\nFill'd it with cursing cries and deep exclaims.\nIf thou delight to view thy heinous deeds,\nBehold this pattern of thy butcheries.\nO, gentlemen, see, see! dead Henry's wounds\nOpen their congeal'd mouths and bleed afresh!\nBlush, Blush, thou lump of foul deformity;\nFor 'tis thy presence that exhales this blood\nFrom cold and empty veins, where no blood dwells;\nThy deed, inhuman and unnatural,\nProvokes this deluge most unnatural.\nO God, which this blood madest, revenge his death!\nO earth, which this blood drink'st revenge his death!\nEither heaven with lightning strike the\nmurderer dead,\nOr earth, gape open wide and eat him quick,\nAs thou dost swallow up this good king's blood\nWhich his hell-govern'd arm hath butchered!","sourceLine":"1.2.50","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lady, you know no rules of charity,\nWhich renders good for bad, blessings for curses.","sourceLine":"1.2.69","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Villain, thou know'st no law of God nor man:\nNo beast so fierce but knows some touch of pity.","sourceLine":"1.2.71","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I know none, and therefore am no beast.","sourceLine":"1.2.73","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O wonderful, when devils tell the truth!","sourceLine":"1.2.74","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"More wonderful, when angels are so angry.\nVouchsafe, divine perfection of a woman,\nOf these supposed-evils, to give me leave,\nBy circumstance, but to acquit myself.","sourceLine":"1.2.75","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Vouchsafe, defused infection of a man,\nFor these known evils, but to give me leave,\nBy circumstance, to curse thy cursed self.","sourceLine":"1.2.79","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fairer than tongue can name thee, let me have\nSome patient leisure to excuse myself.","sourceLine":"1.2.82","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fouler than heart can think thee, thou canst make\nNo excuse current, but to hang thyself.","sourceLine":"1.2.84","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By such despair, I should accuse myself.","sourceLine":"1.2.86","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, by despairing, shouldst thou stand excused;\nFor doing worthy vengeance on thyself,\nWhich didst unworthy slaughter upon others.","sourceLine":"1.2.87","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say that I slew them not?","sourceLine":"1.2.90","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then they are not dead:\nBut dead they are, and devilish slave, by thee.","sourceLine":"1.2.91","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did not kill your husband.","sourceLine":"1.2.93","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then he is alive.","sourceLine":"1.2.94","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, he is dead; and slain by Edward's hand.","sourceLine":"1.2.95","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In thy foul throat thou liest: Queen Margaret saw\nThy murderous falchion smoking in his blood;\nThe which thou once didst bend against her breast,\nBut that thy brothers beat aside the point.","sourceLine":"1.2.96","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I was provoked by her slanderous tongue,\nwhich laid their guilt upon my guiltless shoulders.","sourceLine":"1.2.100","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou wast provoked by thy bloody mind.\nWhich never dreamt on aught but butcheries:\nDidst thou not kill this king?","sourceLine":"1.2.102","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I grant ye.","sourceLine":"1.2.105","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dost grant me, hedgehog? then, God grant me too\nThou mayst be damned for that wicked deed!\nO, he was gentle, mild, and virtuous!","sourceLine":"1.2.106","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The fitter for the King of heaven, that hath him.","sourceLine":"1.2.109","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is in heaven, where thou shalt never come.","sourceLine":"1.2.110","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him thank me, that holp to send him thither;\nFor he was fitter for that place than earth.","sourceLine":"1.2.111","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And thou unfit for any place but hell.","sourceLine":"1.2.113","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, one place else, if you will hear me name it.","sourceLine":"1.2.114","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some dungeon.","sourceLine":"1.2.115","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your bed-chamber.","sourceLine":"1.2.116","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ill rest betide the chamber where thou liest!","sourceLine":"1.2.117","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So will it, madam till I lie with you.","sourceLine":"1.2.118","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope so.","sourceLine":"1.2.119","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know so. But, gentle Lady Anne,\nTo leave this keen encounter of our wits,\nAnd fall somewhat into a slower method,\nIs not the causer of the timeless deaths\nOf these Plantagenets, Henry and Edward,\nAs blameful as the executioner?","sourceLine":"1.2.120","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou art the cause, and most accursed effect.","sourceLine":"1.2.126","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your beauty was the cause of that effect;\nYour beauty: which did haunt me in my sleep\nTo undertake the death of all the world,\nSo I might live one hour in your sweet bosom.","sourceLine":"1.2.127","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I thought that, I tell thee, homicide,\nThese nails should rend that beauty from my cheeks.","sourceLine":"1.2.131","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These eyes could never endure sweet beauty's wreck;\nYou should not blemish it, if I stood by:\nAs all the world is cheered by the sun,\nSo I by that; it is my day, my life.","sourceLine":"1.2.133","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Black night o'ershade thy day, and death thy life!","sourceLine":"1.2.137","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Curse not thyself, fair creature thou art both.","sourceLine":"1.2.138","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I were, to be revenged on thee.","sourceLine":"1.2.139","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is a quarrel most unnatural,\nTo be revenged on him that loveth you.","sourceLine":"1.2.140","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is a quarrel just and reasonable,\nTo be revenged on him that slew my husband.","sourceLine":"1.2.142","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He that bereft thee, lady, of thy husband,\nDid it to help thee to a better husband.","sourceLine":"1.2.144","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His better doth not breathe upon the earth.","sourceLine":"1.2.146","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He lives that loves thee better than he could.","sourceLine":"1.2.147","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Name him.","sourceLine":"1.2.148","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Plantagenet.","sourceLine":"1.2.149","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, that was he.","sourceLine":"1.2.150","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The selfsame name, but one of better nature.","sourceLine":"1.2.151","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is he?","sourceLine":"1.2.152","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here.","sourceLine":"1.2.153","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:60"},{"kind":"stage","text":"She spitteth at him","id":"richardiii:1.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why dost thou spit at me?","sourceLine":"1.2.154","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would it were mortal poison, for thy sake!","sourceLine":"1.2.155","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never came poison from so sweet a place.","sourceLine":"1.2.156","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never hung poison on a fouler toad.\nOut of my sight! thou dost infect my eyes.","sourceLine":"1.2.157","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thine eyes, sweet lady, have infected mine.","sourceLine":"1.2.159","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would they were basilisks, to strike thee dead!","sourceLine":"1.2.160","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would they were, that I might die at once;\nFor now they kill me with a living death.\nThose eyes of thine from mine have drawn salt tears,\nShamed their aspect with store of childish drops:\nThese eyes that never shed remorseful tear,\nNo, when my father York and Edward wept,\nTo hear the piteous moan that Rutland made\nWhen black-faced Clifford shook his sword at him;\nNor when thy warlike father, like a child,\nTold the sad story of my father's death,\nAnd twenty times made pause to sob and weep,\nThat all the standers-by had wet their cheeks\nLike trees bedash'd with rain: in that sad time\nMy manly eyes did scorn an humble tear;\nAnd what these sorrows could not thence exhale,\nThy beauty hath, and made them blind with weeping.\nI never sued to friend nor enemy;\nMy tongue could never learn sweet smoothing word;\nBut now thy beauty is proposed my fee,\nMy proud heart sues, and prompts my tongue to speak.","sourceLine":"1.2.161","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:68"},{"kind":"stage","text":"She looks scornfully at him","id":"richardiii:1.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Teach not thy lips such scorn, for they were made\nFor kissing, lady, not for such contempt.\nIf thy revengeful heart cannot forgive,\nLo, here I lend thee this sharp-pointed sword;\nWhich if thou please to hide in this true bosom.\nAnd let the soul forth that adoreth thee,\nI lay it naked to the deadly stroke,\nAnd humbly beg the death upon my knee.","sourceLine":"1.2.181","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:70"},{"kind":"stage","text":"He lays his breast open: she offers at it with his sword","id":"richardiii:1.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, do not pause; for I did kill King Henry,\nBut 'twas thy beauty that provoked me.\nNay, now dispatch; 'twas I that stabb'd young Edward,\nBut 'twas thy heavenly face that set me on.","sourceLine":"1.2.189","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:72"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Here she lets fall the sword","id":"richardiii:1.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take up the sword again, or take up me.","sourceLine":"1.2.193","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arise, dissembler: though I wish thy death,\nI will not be the executioner.","sourceLine":"1.2.194","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then bid me kill myself, and I will do it.","sourceLine":"1.2.196","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have already.","sourceLine":"1.2.197","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tush, that was in thy rage:\nSpeak it again, and, even with the word,\nThat hand, which, for thy love, did kill thy love,\nShall, for thy love, kill a far truer love;\nTo both their deaths thou shalt be accessary.","sourceLine":"1.2.198","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I knew thy heart.","sourceLine":"1.2.203","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis figured in my tongue.","sourceLine":"1.2.204","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I fear me both are false.","sourceLine":"1.2.205","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then never man was true.","sourceLine":"1.2.206","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, well, put up your sword.","sourceLine":"1.2.207","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, then, my peace is made.","sourceLine":"1.2.208","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That shall you know hereafter.","sourceLine":"1.2.209","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But shall I live in hope?","sourceLine":"1.2.210","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All men, I hope, live so.","sourceLine":"1.2.211","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Vouchsafe to wear this ring.","sourceLine":"1.2.212","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To take is not to give.","sourceLine":"1.2.213","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look, how this ring encompasseth finger.\nEven so thy breast encloseth my poor heart;\nWear both of them, for both of them are thine.\nAnd if thy poor devoted suppliant may\nBut beg one favour at thy gracious hand,\nThou dost confirm his happiness for ever.","sourceLine":"1.2.214","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is it?","sourceLine":"1.2.220","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That it would please thee leave these sad designs\nTo him that hath more cause to be a mourner,\nAnd presently repair to Crosby Place;\nWhere, after I have solemnly interr'd\nAt Chertsey monastery this noble king,\nAnd wet his grave with my repentant tears,\nI will with all expedient duty see you:\nFor divers unknown reasons. I beseech you,\nGrant me this boon.","sourceLine":"1.2.221","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With all my heart; and much it joys me too,\nTo see you are become so penitent.\nTressel and Berkeley, go along with me.","sourceLine":"1.2.230","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bid me farewell.","sourceLine":"1.2.233","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis more than you deserve;\nBut since you teach me how to flatter you,\nImagine I have said farewell already.","sourceLine":"1.2.234","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:1.2:95"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt LADY ANNE, TRESSEL, and BERKELEY","id":"richardiii:1.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirs, take up the corse.","sourceLine":"1.2.237","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Towards Chertsey, noble lord?","sourceLine":"1.2.238","speaker":"GENTLEMEN","id":"richardiii:1.2:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, to White-Friars; there attend my coining.","sourceLine":"1.2.239","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:99"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was ever woman in this humour woo'd?\nWas ever woman in this humour won?\nI'll have her; but I will not keep her long.\nWhat! I, that kill'd her husband and his father,\nTo take her in her heart's extremest hate,\nWith curses in her mouth, tears in her eyes,\nThe bleeding witness of her hatred by;\nHaving God, her conscience, and these bars\nagainst me,\nAnd I nothing to back my suit at all,\nBut the plain devil and dissembling looks,\nAnd yet to win her, all the world to nothing!\nHa!\nHath she forgot already that brave prince,\nEdward, her lord, whom I, some three months since,\nStabb'd in my angry mood at Tewksbury?\nA sweeter and a lovelier gentleman,\nFramed in the prodigality of nature,\nYoung, valiant, wise, and, no doubt, right royal,\nThe spacious world cannot again afford\nAnd will she yet debase her eyes on me,\nThat cropp'd the golden prime of this sweet prince,\nAnd made her widow to a woful bed?\nOn me, whose all not equals Edward's moiety?\nOn me, that halt and am unshapen thus?\nMy dukedom to a beggarly denier,\nI do mistake my person all this while:\nUpon my life, she finds, although I cannot,\nMyself to be a marvellous proper man.\nI'll be at charges for a looking-glass,\nAnd entertain some score or two of tailors,\nTo study fashions to adorn my body:\nSince I am crept in favour with myself,\nWill maintain it with some little cost.\nBut first I'll turn yon fellow in his grave;\nAnd then return lamenting to my love.\nShine out, fair sun, till I have bought a glass,\nThat I may see my shadow as I pass.","sourceLine":"1.2.240","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.2:101"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:1.2:102"}],"scene":2},{"id":"1.3","label":"Act 1 · SCENE III. The palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN ELIZABETH, RIVERS, and GREY","id":"richardiii:1.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have patience, madam: there's no doubt his majesty\nWill soon recover his accustom'd health.","sourceLine":"1.3.1","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In that you brook it in, it makes him worse:\nTherefore, for God's sake, entertain good comfort,\nAnd cheer his grace with quick and merry words.","sourceLine":"1.3.3","speaker":"GREY","id":"richardiii:1.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If he were dead, what would betide of me?","sourceLine":"1.3.6","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No other harm but loss of such a lord.","sourceLine":"1.3.7","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The loss of such a lord includes all harm.","sourceLine":"1.3.8","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The heavens have bless'd you with a goodly son,\nTo be your comforter when he is gone.","sourceLine":"1.3.9","speaker":"GREY","id":"richardiii:1.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh, he is young and his minority\nIs put unto the trust of Richard Gloucester,\nA man that loves not me, nor none of you.","sourceLine":"1.3.11","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it concluded that he shall be protector?","sourceLine":"1.3.14","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is determined, not concluded yet:\nBut so it must be, if the king miscarry.","sourceLine":"1.3.15","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM and DERBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here come the lords of Buckingham and Derby.","sourceLine":"1.3.17","speaker":"GREY","id":"richardiii:1.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good time of day unto your royal grace!","sourceLine":"1.3.18","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God make your majesty joyful as you have been!","sourceLine":"1.3.19","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Countess Richmond, good my Lord of Derby.\nTo your good prayers will scarcely say amen.\nYet, Derby, notwithstanding she's your wife,\nAnd loves not me, be you, good lord, assured\nI hate not you for her proud arrogance.","sourceLine":"1.3.20","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do beseech you, either not believe\nThe envious slanders of her false accusers;\nOr, if she be accused in true report,\nBear with her weakness, which, I think proceeds\nFrom wayward sickness, and no grounded malice.","sourceLine":"1.3.25","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Saw you the king to-day, my Lord of Derby?","sourceLine":"1.3.30","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But now the Duke of Buckingham and I\nAre come from visiting his majesty.","sourceLine":"1.3.31","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What likelihood of his amendment, lords?","sourceLine":"1.3.33","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, good hope; his grace speaks cheerfully.","sourceLine":"1.3.34","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God grant him health! Did you confer with him?","sourceLine":"1.3.35","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, we did: he desires to make atonement\nBetwixt the Duke of Gloucester and your brothers,\nAnd betwixt them and my lord chamberlain;\nAnd sent to warn them to his royal presence.","sourceLine":"1.3.36","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would all were well! but that will never be\nI fear our happiness is at the highest.","sourceLine":"1.3.40","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER, HASTINGS, and DORSET","id":"richardiii:1.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They do me wrong, and I will not endure it:\nWho are they that complain unto the king,\nThat I, forsooth, am stern, and love them not?\nBy holy Paul, they love his grace but lightly\nThat fill his ears with such dissentious rumours.\nBecause I cannot flatter and speak fair,\nSmile in men's faces, smooth, deceive and cog,\nDuck with French nods and apish courtesy,\nI must be held a rancorous enemy.\nCannot a plain man live and think no harm,\nBut thus his simple truth must be abused\nBy silken, sly, insinuating Jacks?","sourceLine":"1.3.42","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To whom in all this presence speaks your grace?","sourceLine":"1.3.54","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To thee, that hast nor honesty nor grace.\nWhen have I injured thee? when done thee wrong?\nOr thee? or thee? or any of your faction?\nA plague upon you all! His royal person,--\nWhom God preserve better than you would wish!--\nCannot be quiet scarce a breathing-while,\nBut you must trouble him with lewd complaints.","sourceLine":"1.3.55","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brother of Gloucester, you mistake the matter.\nThe king, of his own royal disposition,\nAnd not provoked by any suitor else;\nAiming, belike, at your interior hatred,\nWhich in your outward actions shows itself\nAgainst my kindred, brothers, and myself,\nMakes him to send; that thereby he may gather\nThe ground of your ill-will, and so remove it.","sourceLine":"1.3.62","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot tell: the world is grown so bad,\nThat wrens make prey where eagles dare not perch:\nSince every Jack became a gentleman\nThere's many a gentle person made a Jack.","sourceLine":"1.3.70","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, we know your meaning, brother\nGloucester;\nYou envy my advancement and my friends':\nGod grant we never may have need of you!","sourceLine":"1.3.74","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Meantime, God grants that we have need of you:\nYour brother is imprison'd by your means,\nMyself disgraced, and the nobility\nHeld in contempt; whilst many fair promotions\nAre daily given to ennoble those\nThat scarce, some two days since, were worth a noble.","sourceLine":"1.3.78","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By Him that raised me to this careful height\nFrom that contented hap which I enjoy'd,\nI never did incense his majesty\nAgainst the Duke of Clarence, but have been\nAn earnest advocate to plead for him.\nMy lord, you do me shameful injury,\nFalsely to draw me in these vile suspects.","sourceLine":"1.3.84","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You may deny that you were not the cause\nOf my Lord Hastings' late imprisonment.","sourceLine":"1.3.91","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She may, my lord, for--","sourceLine":"1.3.93","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She may, Lord Rivers! why, who knows not so?\nShe may do more, sir, than denying that:\nShe may help you to many fair preferments,\nAnd then deny her aiding hand therein,\nAnd lay those honours on your high deserts.\nWhat may she not? She may, yea, marry, may she--","sourceLine":"1.3.94","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, marry, may she?","sourceLine":"1.3.100","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, marry, may she! marry with a king,\nA bachelor, a handsome stripling too:\nI wis your grandam had a worser match.","sourceLine":"1.3.101","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Gloucester, I have too long borne\nYour blunt upbraidings and your bitter scoffs:\nBy heaven, I will acquaint his majesty\nWith those gross taunts I often have endured.\nI had rather be a country servant-maid\nThan a great queen, with this condition,\nTo be thus taunted, scorn'd, and baited at:","sourceLine":"1.3.104","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN MARGARET, behind","id":"richardiii:1.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Small joy have I in being England's queen.","sourceLine":"1.3.111","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And lessen'd be that small, God, I beseech thee!\nThy honour, state and seat is due to me.","sourceLine":"1.3.112","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What! threat you me with telling of the king?\nTell him, and spare not: look, what I have said\nI will avouch in presence of the king:\nI dare adventure to be sent to the Tower.\n'Tis time to speak; my pains are quite forgot.","sourceLine":"1.3.114","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Out, devil! I remember them too well:\nThou slewest my husband Henry in the Tower,\nAnd Edward, my poor son, at Tewksbury.","sourceLine":"1.3.119","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ere you were queen, yea, or your husband king,\nI was a pack-horse in his great affairs;\nA weeder-out of his proud adversaries,\nA liberal rewarder of his friends:\nTo royalize his blood I spilt mine own.","sourceLine":"1.3.122","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yea, and much better blood than his or thine.","sourceLine":"1.3.127","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In all which time you and your husband Grey\nWere factious for the house of Lancaster;\nAnd, Rivers, so were you. Was not your husband\nIn Margaret's battle at Saint Alban's slain?\nLet me put in your minds, if you forget,\nWhat you have been ere now, and what you are;\nWithal, what I have been, and what I am.","sourceLine":"1.3.128","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A murderous villain, and so still thou art.","sourceLine":"1.3.135","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor Clarence did forsake his father, Warwick;\nYea, and forswore himself,--which Jesu pardon!--","sourceLine":"1.3.136","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which God revenge!","sourceLine":"1.3.138","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To fight on Edward's party for the crown;\nAnd for his meed, poor lord, he is mew'd up.\nI would to God my heart were flint, like Edward's;\nOr Edward's soft and pitiful, like mine\nI am too childish-foolish for this world.","sourceLine":"1.3.139","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hie thee to hell for shame, and leave the world,\nThou cacodemon! there thy kingdom is.","sourceLine":"1.3.144","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Gloucester, in those busy days\nWhich here you urge to prove us enemies,\nWe follow'd then our lord, our lawful king:\nSo should we you, if you should be our king.","sourceLine":"1.3.146","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I should be! I had rather be a pedlar:\nFar be it from my heart, the thought of it!","sourceLine":"1.3.150","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As little joy, my lord, as you suppose\nYou should enjoy, were you this country's king,\nAs little joy may you suppose in me.\nThat I enjoy, being the queen thereof.","sourceLine":"1.3.152","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A little joy enjoys the queen thereof;\nFor I am she, and altogether joyless.\nI can no longer hold me patient.","sourceLine":"1.3.156","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:55"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Advancing","id":"richardiii:1.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear me, you wrangling pirates, that fall out\nIn sharing that which you have pill'd from me!\nWhich of you trembles not that looks on me?\nIf not, that, I being queen, you bow like subjects,\nYet that, by you deposed, you quake like rebels?\nO gentle villain, do not turn away!","sourceLine":"1.3.159","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Foul wrinkled witch, what makest thou in my sight?","sourceLine":"1.3.165","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But repetition of what thou hast marr'd;\nThat will I make before I let thee go.","sourceLine":"1.3.166","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wert thou not banished on pain of death?","sourceLine":"1.3.168","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I was; but I do find more pain in banishment\nThan death can yield me here by my abode.\nA husband and a son thou owest to me;\nAnd thou a kingdom; all of you allegiance:\nThe sorrow that I have, by right is yours,\nAnd all the pleasures you usurp are mine.","sourceLine":"1.3.169","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The curse my noble father laid on thee,\nWhen thou didst crown his warlike brows with paper\nAnd with thy scorns drew'st rivers from his eyes,\nAnd then, to dry them, gavest the duke a clout\nSteep'd in the faultless blood of pretty Rutland--\nHis curses, then from bitterness of soul\nDenounced against thee, are all fall'n upon thee;\nAnd God, not we, hath plagued thy bloody deed.","sourceLine":"1.3.175","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So just is God, to right the innocent.","sourceLine":"1.3.183","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, 'twas the foulest deed to slay that babe,\nAnd the most merciless that e'er was heard of!","sourceLine":"1.3.184","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tyrants themselves wept when it was reported.","sourceLine":"1.3.186","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No man but prophesied revenge for it.","sourceLine":"1.3.187","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:1.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Northumberland, then present, wept to see it.","sourceLine":"1.3.188","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What were you snarling all before I came,\nReady to catch each other by the throat,\nAnd turn you all your hatred now on me?\nDid York's dread curse prevail so much with heaven?\nThat Henry's death, my lovely Edward's death,\nTheir kingdom's loss, my woful banishment,\nCould all but answer for that peevish brat?\nCan curses pierce the clouds and enter heaven?\nWhy, then, give way, dull clouds, to my quick curses!\nIf not by war, by surfeit die your king,\nAs ours by murder, to make him a king!\nEdward thy son, which now is Prince of Wales,\nFor Edward my son, which was Prince of Wales,\nDie in his youth by like untimely violence!\nThyself a queen, for me that was a queen,\nOutlive thy glory, like my wretched self!\nLong mayst thou live to wail thy children's loss;\nAnd see another, as I see thee now,\nDeck'd in thy rights, as thou art stall'd in mine!\nLong die thy happy days before thy death;\nAnd, after many lengthen'd hours of grief,\nDie neither mother, wife, nor England's queen!\nRivers and Dorset, you were standers by,\nAnd so wast thou, Lord Hastings, when my son\nWas stabb'd with bloody daggers: God, I pray him,\nThat none of you may live your natural age,\nBut by some unlook'd accident cut off!","sourceLine":"1.3.189","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have done thy charm, thou hateful wither'd hag!","sourceLine":"1.3.216","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And leave out thee? stay, dog, for thou shalt hear me.\nIf heaven have any grievous plague in store\nExceeding those that I can wish upon thee,\nO, let them keep it till thy sins be ripe,\nAnd then hurl down their indignation\nOn thee, the troubler of the poor world's peace!\nThe worm of conscience still begnaw thy soul!\nThy friends suspect for traitors while thou livest,\nAnd take deep traitors for thy dearest friends!\nNo sleep close up that deadly eye of thine,\nUnless it be whilst some tormenting dream\nAffrights thee with a hell of ugly devils!\nThou elvish-mark'd, abortive, rooting hog!\nThou that wast seal'd in thy nativity\nThe slave of nature and the son of hell!\nThou slander of thy mother's heavy womb!\nThou loathed issue of thy father's loins!\nThou rag of honour! thou detested--","sourceLine":"1.3.217","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Margaret.","sourceLine":"1.3.235","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Richard!","sourceLine":"1.3.236","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ha!","sourceLine":"1.3.237","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I call thee not.","sourceLine":"1.3.238","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cry thee mercy then, for I had thought\nThat thou hadst call'd me all these bitter names.","sourceLine":"1.3.239","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, so I did; but look'd for no reply.\nO, let me make the period to my curse!","sourceLine":"1.3.241","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis done by me, and ends in 'Margaret.'","sourceLine":"1.3.243","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus have you breathed your curse against yourself.","sourceLine":"1.3.244","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor painted queen, vain flourish of my fortune!\nWhy strew'st thou sugar on that bottled spider,\nWhose deadly web ensnareth thee about?\nFool, fool! thou whet'st a knife to kill thyself.\nThe time will come when thou shalt wish for me\nTo help thee curse that poisonous bunchback'd toad.","sourceLine":"1.3.245","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"False-boding woman, end thy frantic curse,\nLest to thy harm thou move our patience.","sourceLine":"1.3.251","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.3:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Foul shame upon you! you have all moved mine.","sourceLine":"1.3.253","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Were you well served, you would be taught your duty.","sourceLine":"1.3.254","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To serve me well, you all should do me duty,\nTeach me to be your queen, and you my subjects:\nO, serve me well, and teach yourselves that duty!","sourceLine":"1.3.255","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dispute not with her; she is lunatic.","sourceLine":"1.3.258","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:1.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, master marquess, you are malapert:\nYour fire-new stamp of honour is scarce current.\nO, that your young nobility could judge\nWhat 'twere to lose it, and be miserable!\nThey that stand high have many blasts to shake them;\nAnd if they fall, they dash themselves to pieces.","sourceLine":"1.3.259","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good counsel, marry: learn it, learn it, marquess.","sourceLine":"1.3.265","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It toucheth you, my lord, as much as me.","sourceLine":"1.3.266","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:1.3:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yea, and much more: but I was born so high,\nOur aery buildeth in the cedar's top,\nAnd dallies with the wind and scorns the sun.","sourceLine":"1.3.267","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And turns the sun to shade; alas! alas!\nWitness my son, now in the shade of death;\nWhose bright out-shining beams thy cloudy wrath\nHath in eternal darkness folded up.\nYour aery buildeth in our aery's nest.\nO God, that seest it, do not suffer it!\nAs it was won with blood, lost be it so!","sourceLine":"1.3.270","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have done! for shame, if not for charity.","sourceLine":"1.3.277","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Urge neither charity nor shame to me:\nUncharitably with me have you dealt,\nAnd shamefully by you my hopes are butcher'd.\nMy charity is outrage, life my shame\nAnd in that shame still live my sorrow's rage.","sourceLine":"1.3.278","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have done, have done.","sourceLine":"1.3.283","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O princely Buckingham I'll kiss thy hand,\nIn sign of league and amity with thee:\nNow fair befal thee and thy noble house!\nThy garments are not spotted with our blood,\nNor thou within the compass of my curse.","sourceLine":"1.3.284","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor no one here; for curses never pass\nThe lips of those that breathe them in the air.","sourceLine":"1.3.289","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll not believe but they ascend the sky,\nAnd there awake God's gentle-sleeping peace.\nO Buckingham, take heed of yonder dog!\nLook, when he fawns, he bites; and when he bites,\nHis venom tooth will rankle to the death:\nHave not to do with him, beware of him;\nSin, death, and hell have set their marks on him,\nAnd all their ministers attend on him.","sourceLine":"1.3.291","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What doth she say, my Lord of Buckingham?","sourceLine":"1.3.299","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing that I respect, my gracious lord.","sourceLine":"1.3.300","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:1.3:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, dost thou scorn me for my gentle counsel?\nAnd soothe the devil that I warn thee from?\nO, but remember this another day,\nWhen he shall split thy very heart with sorrow,\nAnd say poor Margaret was a prophetess!\nLive each of you the subjects to his hate,\nAnd he to yours, and all of you to God's!","sourceLine":"1.3.301","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:1.3:98"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:1.3:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My hair doth stand on end to hear her curses.","sourceLine":"1.3.308","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:1.3:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so doth mine: I muse why she's at liberty.","sourceLine":"1.3.309","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot blame her: by God's holy mother,\nShe hath had too much wrong; and I repent\nMy part thereof that I have done to her.","sourceLine":"1.3.310","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I never did her any, to my knowledge.","sourceLine":"1.3.313","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But you have all the vantage of her wrong.\nI was too hot to do somebody good,\nThat is too cold in thinking of it now.\nMarry, as for Clarence, he is well repaid,\nHe is frank'd up to fatting for his pains\nGod pardon them that are the cause of it!","sourceLine":"1.3.314","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A virtuous and a Christian-like conclusion,\nTo pray for them that have done scathe to us.","sourceLine":"1.3.320","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So do I ever:","sourceLine":"1.3.322","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:106"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside","id":"richardiii:1.3:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"being well-advised.\nFor had I cursed now, I had cursed myself.","sourceLine":"1.3.323","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:108"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, his majesty doth call for you,\nAnd for your grace; and you, my noble lords.","sourceLine":"1.3.325","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:1.3:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Catesby, we come. Lords, will you go with us?","sourceLine":"1.3.327","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:1.3:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, we will attend your grace.","sourceLine":"1.3.328","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:1.3:112"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do the wrong, and first begin to brawl.\nThe secret mischiefs that I set abroach\nI lay unto the grievous charge of others.\nClarence, whom I, indeed, have laid in darkness,\nI do beweep to many simple gulls\nNamely, to Hastings, Derby, Buckingham;\nAnd say it is the queen and her allies\nThat stir the king against the duke my brother.\nNow, they believe it; and withal whet me\nTo be revenged on Rivers, Vaughan, Grey:\nBut then I sigh; and, with a piece of scripture,\nTell them that God bids us do good for evil:\nAnd thus I clothe my naked villany\nWith old odd ends stolen out of holy writ;\nAnd seem a saint, when most I play the devil.","sourceLine":"1.3.329","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:114"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter two Murderers","id":"richardiii:1.3:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But, soft! here come my executioners.\nHow now, my hardy, stout resolved mates!\nAre you now going to dispatch this deed?","sourceLine":"1.3.344","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We are, my lord; and come to have the warrant\nThat we may be admitted where he is.","sourceLine":"1.3.347","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.3:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well thought upon; I have it here about me.","sourceLine":"1.3.349","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:118"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Gives the warrant","id":"richardiii:1.3:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When you have done, repair to Crosby Place.\nBut, sirs, be sudden in the execution,\nWithal obdurate, do not hear him plead;\nFor Clarence is well-spoken, and perhaps\nMay move your hearts to pity if you mark him.","sourceLine":"1.3.350","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tush!\nFear not, my lord, we will not stand to prate;\nTalkers are no good doers: be assured\nWe come to use our hands and not our tongues.","sourceLine":"1.3.355","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.3:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your eyes drop millstones, when fools' eyes drop tears:\nI like you, lads; about your business straight;\nGo, go, dispatch.","sourceLine":"1.3.359","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:1.3:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We will, my noble lord.","sourceLine":"1.3.362","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.3:123"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:1.3:124"}],"scene":3},{"id":"1.4","label":"Act 1 · SCENE IV. London. The Tower.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLARENCE and BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why looks your grace so heavily today?","sourceLine":"1.4.1","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I have pass'd a miserable night,\nSo full of ugly sights, of ghastly dreams,\nThat, as I am a Christian faithful man,\nI would not spend another such a night,\nThough 'twere to buy a world of happy days,\nSo full of dismal terror was the time!","sourceLine":"1.4.2","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What was your dream? I long to hear you tell it.","sourceLine":"1.4.8","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methoughts that I had broken from the Tower,\nAnd was embark'd to cross to Burgundy;\nAnd, in my company, my brother Gloucester;\nWho from my cabin tempted me to walk\nUpon the hatches: thence we looked toward England,\nAnd cited up a thousand fearful times,\nDuring the wars of York and Lancaster\nThat had befall'n us. As we paced along\nUpon the giddy footing of the hatches,\nMethought that Gloucester stumbled; and, in falling,\nStruck me, that thought to stay him, overboard,\nInto the tumbling billows of the main.\nLord, Lord! methought, what pain it was to drown!\nWhat dreadful noise of waters in mine ears!\nWhat ugly sights of death within mine eyes!\nMethought I saw a thousand fearful wrecks;\nTen thousand men that fishes gnaw'd upon;\nWedges of gold, great anchors, heaps of pearl,\nInestimable stones, unvalued jewels,\nAll scatter'd in the bottom of the sea:\nSome lay in dead men's skulls; and, in those holes\nWhere eyes did once inhabit, there were crept,\nAs 'twere in scorn of eyes, reflecting gems,\nWhich woo'd the slimy bottom of the deep,\nAnd mock'd the dead bones that lay scatter'd by.","sourceLine":"1.4.9","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Had you such leisure in the time of death\nTo gaze upon the secrets of the deep?","sourceLine":"1.4.34","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methought I had; and often did I strive\nTo yield the ghost: but still the envious flood\nKept in my soul, and would not let it forth\nTo seek the empty, vast and wandering air;\nBut smother'd it within my panting bulk,\nWhich almost burst to belch it in the sea.","sourceLine":"1.4.36","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Awaked you not with this sore agony?","sourceLine":"1.4.42","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, no, my dream was lengthen'd after life;\nO, then began the tempest to my soul,\nWho pass'd, methought, the melancholy flood,\nWith that grim ferryman which poets write of,\nUnto the kingdom of perpetual night.\nThe first that there did greet my stranger soul,\nWas my great father-in-law, renowned Warwick;\nWho cried aloud, 'What scourge for perjury\nCan this dark monarchy afford false Clarence?'\nAnd so he vanish'd: then came wandering by\nA shadow like an angel, with bright hair\nDabbled in blood; and he squeak'd out aloud,\n'Clarence is come; false, fleeting, perjured Clarence,\nThat stabb'd me in the field by Tewksbury;\nSeize on him, Furies, take him to your torments!'\nWith that, methoughts, a legion of foul fiends\nEnviron'd me about, and howled in mine ears\nSuch hideous cries, that with the very noise\nI trembling waked, and for a season after\nCould not believe but that I was in hell,\nSuch terrible impression made the dream.","sourceLine":"1.4.43","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No marvel, my lord, though it affrighted you;\nI promise, I am afraid to hear you tell it.","sourceLine":"1.4.64","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Brakenbury, I have done those things,\nWhich now bear evidence against my soul,\nFor Edward's sake; and see how he requites me!\nO God! if my deep prayers cannot appease thee,\nBut thou wilt be avenged on my misdeeds,\nYet execute thy wrath in me alone,\nO, spare my guiltless wife and my poor children!\nI pray thee, gentle keeper, stay by me;\nMy soul is heavy, and I fain would sleep.","sourceLine":"1.4.66","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will, my lord: God give your grace good rest!","sourceLine":"1.4.75","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"CLARENCE sleeps","id":"richardiii:1.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sorrow breaks seasons and reposing hours,\nMakes the night morning, and the noon-tide night.\nPrinces have but their tides for their glories,\nAn outward honour for an inward toil;\nAnd, for unfelt imagination,\nThey often feel a world of restless cares:\nSo that, betwixt their tides and low names,\nThere's nothing differs but the outward fame.","sourceLine":"1.4.76","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the two Murderers","id":"richardiii:1.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ho! who's here?","sourceLine":"1.4.84","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In God's name what are you, and how came you hither?","sourceLine":"1.4.85","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would speak with Clarence, and I came hither on my legs.","sourceLine":"1.4.86","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yea, are you so brief?","sourceLine":"1.4.87","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sir, it is better to be brief than tedious. Show\nhim our commission; talk no more.","sourceLine":"1.4.88","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"BRAKENBURY reads it","id":"richardiii:1.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am, in this, commanded to deliver\nThe noble Duke of Clarence to your hands:\nI will not reason what is meant hereby,\nBecause I will be guiltless of the meaning.\nHere are the keys, there sits the duke asleep:\nI'll to the king; and signify to him\nThat thus I have resign'd my charge to you.","sourceLine":"1.4.90","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do so, it is a point of wisdom: fare you well.","sourceLine":"1.4.97","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:1.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, shall we stab him as he sleeps?","sourceLine":"1.4.98","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No; then he will say 'twas done cowardly, when he wakes.","sourceLine":"1.4.99","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When he wakes! why, fool, he shall never wake till\nthe judgment-day.","sourceLine":"1.4.100","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then he will say we stabbed him sleeping.","sourceLine":"1.4.102","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The urging of that word 'judgment' hath bred a kind\nof remorse in me.","sourceLine":"1.4.103","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, art thou afraid?","sourceLine":"1.4.105","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not to kill him, having a warrant for it; but to be\ndamned for killing him, from which no warrant can defend us.","sourceLine":"1.4.106","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thought thou hadst been resolute.","sourceLine":"1.4.108","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So I am, to let him live.","sourceLine":"1.4.109","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Back to the Duke of Gloucester, tell him so.","sourceLine":"1.4.110","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray thee, stay a while: I hope my holy humour\nwill change; 'twas wont to hold me but while one\nwould tell twenty.","sourceLine":"1.4.111","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How dost thou feel thyself now?","sourceLine":"1.4.114","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Faith, some certain dregs of conscience are yet\nwithin me.","sourceLine":"1.4.115","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Remember our reward, when the deed is done.","sourceLine":"1.4.117","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Zounds, he dies: I had forgot the reward.","sourceLine":"1.4.118","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is thy conscience now?","sourceLine":"1.4.119","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In the Duke of Gloucester's purse.","sourceLine":"1.4.120","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So when he opens his purse to give us our reward,\nthy conscience flies out.","sourceLine":"1.4.121","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let it go; there's few or none will entertain it.","sourceLine":"1.4.123","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How if it come to thee again?","sourceLine":"1.4.124","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll not meddle with it: it is a dangerous thing: it\nmakes a man a coward: a man cannot steal, but it\naccuseth him; he cannot swear, but it cheques him;\nhe cannot lie with his neighbour's wife, but it\ndetects him: 'tis a blushing shamefast spirit that\nmutinies in a man's bosom; it fills one full of\nobstacles: it made me once restore a purse of gold\nthat I found; it beggars any man that keeps it: it\nis turned out of all towns and cities for a\ndangerous thing; and every man that means to live\nwell endeavours to trust to himself and to live\nwithout it.","sourceLine":"1.4.125","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Zounds, it is even now at my elbow, persuading me\nnot to kill the duke.","sourceLine":"1.4.137","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take the devil in thy mind, and relieve him not: he\nwould insinuate with thee but to make thee sigh.","sourceLine":"1.4.139","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, I am strong-framed, he cannot prevail with me,\nI warrant thee.","sourceLine":"1.4.141","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Spoke like a tail fellow that respects his\nreputation. Come, shall we to this gear?","sourceLine":"1.4.143","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take him over the costard with the hilts of thy\nsword, and then we will chop him in the malmsey-butt\nin the next room.","sourceLine":"1.4.145","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O excellent devise! make a sop of him.","sourceLine":"1.4.148","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark! he stirs: shall I strike?","sourceLine":"1.4.149","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, first let's reason with him.","sourceLine":"1.4.150","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where art thou, keeper? give me a cup of wine.","sourceLine":"1.4.151","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall have wine enough, my lord, anon.","sourceLine":"1.4.152","speaker":"Second murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In God's name, what art thou?","sourceLine":"1.4.153","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A man, as you are.","sourceLine":"1.4.154","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But not, as I am, royal.","sourceLine":"1.4.155","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor you, as we are, loyal.","sourceLine":"1.4.156","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy voice is thunder, but thy looks are humble.","sourceLine":"1.4.157","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My voice is now the king's, my looks mine own.","sourceLine":"1.4.158","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How darkly and how deadly dost thou speak!\nYour eyes do menace me: why look you pale?\nWho sent you hither? Wherefore do you come?","sourceLine":"1.4.159","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To, to, to--","sourceLine":"1.4.162","speaker":"Both","id":"richardiii:1.4:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To murder me?","sourceLine":"1.4.163","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, ay.","sourceLine":"1.4.164","speaker":"Both","id":"richardiii:1.4:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You scarcely have the hearts to tell me so,\nAnd therefore cannot have the hearts to do it.\nWherein, my friends, have I offended you?","sourceLine":"1.4.165","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Offended us you have not, but the king.","sourceLine":"1.4.168","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall be reconciled to him again.","sourceLine":"1.4.169","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never, my lord; therefore prepare to die.","sourceLine":"1.4.170","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you call'd forth from out a world of men\nTo slay the innocent? What is my offence?\nWhere are the evidence that do accuse me?\nWhat lawful quest have given their verdict up\nUnto the frowning judge? or who pronounced\nThe bitter sentence of poor Clarence' death?\nBefore I be convict by course of law,\nTo threaten me with death is most unlawful.\nI charge you, as you hope to have redemption\nBy Christ's dear blood shed for our grievous sins,\nThat you depart and lay no hands on me\nThe deed you undertake is damnable.","sourceLine":"1.4.171","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What we will do, we do upon command.","sourceLine":"1.4.183","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And he that hath commanded is the king.","sourceLine":"1.4.184","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Erroneous vassal! the great King of kings\nHath in the tables of his law commanded\nThat thou shalt do no murder: and wilt thou, then,\nSpurn at his edict and fulfil a man's?\nTake heed; for he holds vengeance in his hands,\nTo hurl upon their heads that break his law.","sourceLine":"1.4.185","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And that same vengeance doth he hurl on thee,\nFor false forswearing and for murder too:\nThou didst receive the holy sacrament,\nTo fight in quarrel of the house of Lancaster.","sourceLine":"1.4.191","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, like a traitor to the name of God,\nDidst break that vow; and with thy treacherous blade\nUnrip'dst the bowels of thy sovereign's son.","sourceLine":"1.4.195","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whom thou wert sworn to cherish and defend.","sourceLine":"1.4.198","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How canst thou urge God's dreadful law to us,\nWhen thou hast broke it in so dear degree?","sourceLine":"1.4.199","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas! for whose sake did I that ill deed?\nFor Edward, for my brother, for his sake: Why, sirs,\nHe sends ye not to murder me for this\nFor in this sin he is as deep as I.\nIf God will be revenged for this deed.\nO, know you yet, he doth it publicly,\nTake not the quarrel from his powerful arm;\nHe needs no indirect nor lawless course\nTo cut off those that have offended him.","sourceLine":"1.4.201","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who made thee, then, a bloody minister,\nWhen gallant-springing brave Plantagenet,\nThat princely novice, was struck dead by thee?","sourceLine":"1.4.210","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My brother's love, the devil, and my rage.","sourceLine":"1.4.213","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy brother's love, our duty, and thy fault,\nProvoke us hither now to slaughter thee.","sourceLine":"1.4.214","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh, if you love my brother, hate not me;\nI am his brother, and I love him well.\nIf you be hired for meed, go back again,\nAnd I will send you to my brother Gloucester,\nWho shall reward you better for my life\nThan Edward will for tidings of my death.","sourceLine":"1.4.216","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are deceived, your brother Gloucester hates you.","sourceLine":"1.4.222","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, no, he loves me, and he holds me dear:\nGo you to him from me.","sourceLine":"1.4.223","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, so we will.","sourceLine":"1.4.225","speaker":"Both","id":"richardiii:1.4:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell him, when that our princely father York\nBless'd his three sons with his victorious arm,\nAnd charged us from his soul to love each other,\nHe little thought of this divided friendship:\nBid Gloucester think of this, and he will weep.","sourceLine":"1.4.226","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, millstones; as be lesson'd us to weep.","sourceLine":"1.4.231","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, do not slander him, for he is kind.","sourceLine":"1.4.232","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Right,\nAs snow in harvest. Thou deceivest thyself:\n'Tis he that sent us hither now to slaughter thee.","sourceLine":"1.4.233","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It cannot be; for when I parted with him,\nHe hugg'd me in his arms, and swore, with sobs,\nThat he would labour my delivery.","sourceLine":"1.4.236","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, so he doth, now he delivers thee\nFrom this world's thraldom to the joys of heaven.","sourceLine":"1.4.239","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make peace with God, for you must die, my lord.","sourceLine":"1.4.241","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hast thou that holy feeling in thy soul,\nTo counsel me to make my peace with God,\nAnd art thou yet to thy own soul so blind,\nThat thou wilt war with God by murdering me?\nAh, sirs, consider, he that set you on\nTo do this deed will hate you for the deed.","sourceLine":"1.4.242","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What shall we do?","sourceLine":"1.4.248","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Relent, and save your souls.","sourceLine":"1.4.249","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Relent! 'tis cowardly and womanish.","sourceLine":"1.4.250","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not to relent is beastly, savage, devilish.\nWhich of you, if you were a prince's son,\nBeing pent from liberty, as I am now,\nif two such murderers as yourselves came to you,\nWould not entreat for life?\nMy friend, I spy some pity in thy looks:\nO, if thine eye be not a flatterer,\nCome thou on my side, and entreat for me,\nAs you would beg, were you in my distress\nA begging prince what beggar pities not?","sourceLine":"1.4.251","speaker":"CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:1.4:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look behind you, my lord.","sourceLine":"1.4.261","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take that, and that: if all this will not do,","sourceLine":"1.4.262","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:99"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Stabs him","id":"richardiii:1.4:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll drown you in the malmsey-butt within.","sourceLine":"1.4.263","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:101"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, with the body","id":"richardiii:1.4:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A bloody deed, and desperately dispatch'd!\nHow fain, like Pilate, would I wash my hands\nOf this most grievous guilty murder done!","sourceLine":"1.4.264","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:103"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! what mean'st thou, that thou help'st me not?\nBy heavens, the duke shall know how slack thou art!","sourceLine":"1.4.267","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would he knew that I had saved his brother!\nTake thou the fee, and tell him what I say;\nFor I repent me that the duke is slain.","sourceLine":"1.4.269","speaker":"Second Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:106"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:1.4:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So do not I: go, coward as thou art.\nNow must I hide his body in some hole,\nUntil the duke take order for his burial:\nAnd when I have my meed, I must away;\nFor this will out, and here I must not stay.","sourceLine":"1.4.272","speaker":"First Murderer","id":"richardiii:1.4:108"}],"scene":4},{"id":"2.1","label":"Act 2 · SCENE I. London. The palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter KING EDWARD IV sick, QUEEN ELIZABETH, DORSET, RIVERS, HASTINGS, BUCKINGHAM, GREY, and others","id":"richardiii:2.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, so: now have I done a good day's work:\nYou peers, continue this united league:\nI every day expect an embassage\nFrom my Redeemer to redeem me hence;\nAnd now in peace my soul shall part to heaven,\nSince I have set my friends at peace on earth.\nRivers and Hastings, take each other's hand;\nDissemble not your hatred, swear your love.","sourceLine":"2.1.1","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By heaven, my heart is purged from grudging hate:\nAnd with my hand I seal my true heart's love.","sourceLine":"2.1.9","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So thrive I, as I truly swear the like!","sourceLine":"2.1.11","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:2.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take heed you dally not before your king;\nLest he that is the supreme King of kings\nConfound your hidden falsehood, and award\nEither of you to be the other's end.","sourceLine":"2.1.12","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So prosper I, as I swear perfect love!","sourceLine":"2.1.16","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:2.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I, as I love Hastings with my heart!","sourceLine":"2.1.17","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, yourself are not exempt in this,\nNor your son Dorset, Buckingham, nor you;\nYou have been factious one against the other,\nWife, love Lord Hastings, let him kiss your hand;\nAnd what you do, do it unfeignedly.","sourceLine":"2.1.18","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, Hastings; I will never more remember\nOur former hatred, so thrive I and mine!","sourceLine":"2.1.23","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dorset, embrace him; Hastings, love lord marquess.","sourceLine":"2.1.25","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This interchange of love, I here protest,\nUpon my part shall be unviolable.","sourceLine":"2.1.26","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:2.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so swear I, my lord","sourceLine":"2.1.28","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:2.1:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They embrace","id":"richardiii:2.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, princely Buckingham, seal thou this league\nWith thy embracements to my wife's allies,\nAnd make me happy in your unity.","sourceLine":"2.1.29","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whenever Buckingham doth turn his hate\nOn you or yours,","sourceLine":"2.1.32","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.1:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To the Queen","id":"richardiii:2.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"but with all duteous love\nDoth cherish you and yours, God punish me\nWith hate in those where I expect most love!\nWhen I have most need to employ a friend,\nAnd most assured that he is a friend\nDeep, hollow, treacherous, and full of guile,\nBe he unto me! this do I beg of God,\nWhen I am cold in zeal to yours.","sourceLine":"2.1.34","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A pleasing cordial, princely Buckingham,\nis this thy vow unto my sickly heart.\nThere wanteth now our brother Gloucester here,\nTo make the perfect period of this peace.","sourceLine":"2.1.42","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, in good time, here comes the noble duke.","sourceLine":"2.1.46","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.1:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow to my sovereign king and queen:\nAnd, princely peers, a happy time of day!","sourceLine":"2.1.47","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Happy, indeed, as we have spent the day.\nBrother, we done deeds of charity;\nMade peace enmity, fair love of hate,\nBetween these swelling wrong-incensed peers.","sourceLine":"2.1.49","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A blessed labour, my most sovereign liege:\nAmongst this princely heap, if any here,\nBy false intelligence, or wrong surmise,\nHold me a foe;\nIf I unwittingly, or in my rage,\nHave aught committed that is hardly borne\nBy any in this presence, I desire\nTo reconcile me to his friendly peace:\n'Tis death to me to be at enmity;\nI hate it, and desire all good men's love.\nFirst, madam, I entreat true peace of you,\nWhich I will purchase with my duteous service;\nOf you, my noble cousin Buckingham,\nIf ever any grudge were lodged between us;\nOf you, Lord Rivers, and, Lord Grey, of you;\nThat without desert have frown'd on me;\nDukes, earls, lords, gentlemen; indeed, of all.\nI do not know that Englishman alive\nWith whom my soul is any jot at odds\nMore than the infant that is born to-night\nI thank my God for my humility.","sourceLine":"2.1.53","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A holy day shall this be kept hereafter:\nI would to God all strifes were well compounded.\nMy sovereign liege, I do beseech your majesty\nTo take our brother Clarence to your grace.","sourceLine":"2.1.74","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, madam, have I offer'd love for this\nTo be so bouted in this royal presence?\nWho knows not that the noble duke is dead?","sourceLine":"2.1.78","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They all start","id":"richardiii:2.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You do him injury to scorn his corse.","sourceLine":"2.1.81","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who knows not he is dead! who knows he is?","sourceLine":"2.1.82","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All seeing heaven, what a world is this!","sourceLine":"2.1.83","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look I so pale, Lord Dorset, as the rest?","sourceLine":"2.1.84","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lord; and no one in this presence\nBut his red colour hath forsook his cheeks.","sourceLine":"2.1.85","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:2.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is Clarence dead? the order was reversed.","sourceLine":"2.1.87","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But he, poor soul, by your first order died,\nAnd that a winged Mercury did bear:\nSome tardy cripple bore the countermand,\nThat came too lag to see him buried.\nGod grant that some, less noble and less loyal,\nNearer in bloody thoughts, but not in blood,\nDeserve not worse than wretched Clarence did,\nAnd yet go current from suspicion!","sourceLine":"2.1.88","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DERBY","id":"richardiii:2.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A boon, my sovereign, for my service done!","sourceLine":"2.1.96","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:2.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray thee, peace: my soul is full of sorrow.","sourceLine":"2.1.97","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will not rise, unless your highness grant.","sourceLine":"2.1.98","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:2.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then speak at once what is it thou demand'st.","sourceLine":"2.1.99","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The forfeit, sovereign, of my servant's life;\nWho slew to-day a righteous gentleman\nLately attendant on the Duke of Norfolk.","sourceLine":"2.1.100","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:2.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have a tongue to doom my brother's death,\nAnd shall the same give pardon to a slave?\nMy brother slew no man; his fault was thought,\nAnd yet his punishment was cruel death.\nWho sued to me for him? who, in my rage,\nKneel'd at my feet, and bade me be advised\nWho spake of brotherhood? who spake of love?\nWho told me how the poor soul did forsake\nThe mighty Warwick, and did fight for me?\nWho told me, in the field by Tewksbury\nWhen Oxford had me down, he rescued me,\nAnd said, 'Dear brother, live, and be a king'?\nWho told me, when we both lay in the field\nFrozen almost to death, how he did lap me\nEven in his own garments, and gave himself,\nAll thin and naked, to the numb cold night?\nAll this from my remembrance brutish wrath\nSinfully pluck'd, and not a man of you\nHad so much grace to put it in my mind.\nBut when your carters or your waiting-vassals\nHave done a drunken slaughter, and defaced\nThe precious image of our dear Redeemer,\nYou straight are on your knees for pardon, pardon;\nAnd I unjustly too, must grant it you\nBut for my brother not a man would speak,\nNor I, ungracious, speak unto myself\nFor him, poor soul. The proudest of you all\nHave been beholding to him in his life;\nYet none of you would once plead for his life.\nO God, I fear thy justice will take hold\nOn me, and you, and mine, and yours for this!\nCome, Hastings, help me to my closet.\nOh, poor Clarence!","sourceLine":"2.1.103","speaker":"KING EDWARD IV","id":"richardiii:2.1:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt some with KING EDWARD IV and QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:2.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the fruit of rashness! Mark'd you not\nHow that the guilty kindred of the queen\nLook'd pale when they did hear of Clarence' death?\nO, they did urge it still unto the king!\nGod will revenge it. But come, let us in,\nTo comfort Edward with our company.","sourceLine":"2.1.136","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We wait upon your grace.","sourceLine":"2.1.142","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.1:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:2.1:44"}],"scene":1},{"id":"2.2","label":"Act 2 · SCENE II. The palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the DUCHESS OF YORK, with the two children of CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:2.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me, good grandam, is our father dead?","sourceLine":"2.2.1","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, boy.","sourceLine":"2.2.2","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you wring your hands, and beat your breast,\nAnd cry 'O Clarence, my unhappy son!'","sourceLine":"2.2.3","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you look on us, and shake your head,\nAnd call us wretches, orphans, castaways\nIf that our noble father be alive?","sourceLine":"2.2.5","speaker":"Girl","id":"richardiii:2.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My pretty cousins, you mistake me much;\nI do lament the sickness of the king.\nAs loath to lose him, not your father's death;\nIt were lost sorrow to wail one that's lost.","sourceLine":"2.2.8","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, grandam, you conclude that he is dead.\nThe king my uncle is to blame for this:\nGod will revenge it; whom I will importune\nWith daily prayers all to that effect.","sourceLine":"2.2.12","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so will I.","sourceLine":"2.2.16","speaker":"Girl","id":"richardiii:2.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, children, peace! the king doth love you well:\nIncapable and shallow innocents,\nYou cannot guess who caused your father's death.","sourceLine":"2.2.17","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Grandam, we can; for my good uncle Gloucester\nTold me, the king, provoked by the queen,\nDevised impeachments to imprison him :\nAnd when my uncle told me so, he wept,\nAnd hugg'd me in his arm, and kindly kiss'd my cheek;\nBade me rely on him as on my father,\nAnd he would love me dearly as his child.","sourceLine":"2.2.20","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh, that deceit should steal such gentle shapes,\nAnd with a virtuous vizard hide foul guile!\nHe is my son; yea, and therein my shame;\nYet from my dugs he drew not this deceit.","sourceLine":"2.2.27","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Think you my uncle did dissemble, grandam?","sourceLine":"2.2.31","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, boy.","sourceLine":"2.2.32","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot think it. Hark! what noise is this?","sourceLine":"2.2.33","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN ELIZABETH, with her hair about her ears; RIVERS, and DORSET after her","id":"richardiii:2.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh, who shall hinder me to wail and weep,\nTo chide my fortune, and torment myself?\nI'll join with black despair against my soul,\nAnd to myself become an enemy.","sourceLine":"2.2.34","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What means this scene of rude impatience?","sourceLine":"2.2.38","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To make an act of tragic violence:\nEdward, my lord, your son, our king, is dead.\nWhy grow the branches now the root is wither'd?\nWhy wither not the leaves the sap being gone?\nIf you will live, lament; if die, be brief,\nThat our swift-winged souls may catch the king's;\nOr, like obedient subjects, follow him\nTo his new kingdom of perpetual rest.","sourceLine":"2.2.39","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, so much interest have I in thy sorrow\nAs I had title in thy noble husband!\nI have bewept a worthy husband's death,\nAnd lived by looking on his images:\nBut now two mirrors of his princely semblance\nAre crack'd in pieces by malignant death,\nAnd I for comfort have but one false glass,\nWhich grieves me when I see my shame in him.\nThou art a widow; yet thou art a mother,\nAnd hast the comfort of thy children left thee:\nBut death hath snatch'd my husband from mine arms,\nAnd pluck'd two crutches from my feeble limbs,\nEdward and Clarence. O, what cause have I,\nThine being but a moiety of my grief,\nTo overgo thy plaints and drown thy cries!","sourceLine":"2.2.47","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good aunt, you wept not for our father's death;\nHow can we aid you with our kindred tears?","sourceLine":"2.2.62","speaker":"Boy","id":"richardiii:2.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our fatherless distress was left unmoan'd;\nYour widow-dolour likewise be unwept!","sourceLine":"2.2.64","speaker":"Girl","id":"richardiii:2.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me no help in lamentation;\nI am not barren to bring forth complaints\nAll springs reduce their currents to mine eyes,\nThat I, being govern'd by the watery moon,\nMay send forth plenteous tears to drown the world!\nOh for my husband, for my dear lord Edward!","sourceLine":"2.2.66","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oh for our father, for our dear lord Clarence!","sourceLine":"2.2.72","speaker":"Children","id":"richardiii:2.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas for both, both mine, Edward and Clarence!","sourceLine":"2.2.73","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stay had I but Edward? and he's gone.","sourceLine":"2.2.74","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stay had we but Clarence? and he's gone.","sourceLine":"2.2.75","speaker":"Children","id":"richardiii:2.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What stays had I but they? and they are gone.","sourceLine":"2.2.76","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was never widow had so dear a loss!","sourceLine":"2.2.77","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Were never orphans had so dear a loss!","sourceLine":"2.2.78","speaker":"Children","id":"richardiii:2.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was never mother had so dear a loss!\nAlas, I am the mother of these moans!\nTheir woes are parcell'd, mine are general.\nShe for an Edward weeps, and so do I;\nI for a Clarence weep, so doth not she:\nThese babes for Clarence weep and so do I;\nI for an Edward weep, so do not they:\nAlas, you three, on me, threefold distress'd,\nPour all your tears! I am your sorrow's nurse,\nAnd I will pamper it with lamentations.","sourceLine":"2.2.79","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Comfort, dear mother: God is much displeased\nThat you take with unthankfulness, his doing:\nIn common worldly things, 'tis call'd ungrateful,\nWith dull unwilligness to repay a debt\nWhich with a bounteous hand was kindly lent;\nMuch more to be thus opposite with heaven,\nFor it requires the royal debt it lent you.","sourceLine":"2.2.89","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:2.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, bethink you, like a careful mother,\nOf the young prince your son: send straight for him\nLet him be crown'd; in him your comfort lives:\nDrown desperate sorrow in dead Edward's grave,\nAnd plant your joys in living Edward's throne.","sourceLine":"2.2.96","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.2:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER, BUCKINGHAM, DERBY, HASTINGS, and RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:2.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, have comfort: all of us have cause\nTo wail the dimming of our shining star;\nBut none can cure their harms by wailing them.\nMadam, my mother, I do cry you mercy;\nI did not see your grace: humbly on my knee\nI crave your blessing.","sourceLine":"2.2.101","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God bless thee; and put meekness in thy mind,\nLove, charity, obedience, and true duty!","sourceLine":"2.2.107","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Amen; and make me die a good old man!\nThat is the butt-end of a mother's blessing:\nI marvel why her grace did leave it out.","sourceLine":"2.2.109","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You cloudy princes and heart-sorrowing peers,\nThat bear this mutual heavy load of moan,\nNow cheer each other in each other's love\nThough we have spent our harvest of this king,\nWe are to reap the harvest of his son.\nThe broken rancour of your high-swoln hearts,\nBut lately splinter'd, knit, and join'd together,\nMust gently be preserved, cherish'd, and kept:\nMe seemeth good, that, with some little train,\nForthwith from Ludlow the young prince be fetch'd\nHither to London, to be crown'd our king.","sourceLine":"2.2.112","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why with some little train, my Lord of Buckingham?","sourceLine":"2.2.123","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, my lord, lest, by a multitude,\nThe new-heal'd wound of malice should break out,\nWhich would be so much the more dangerous\nBy how much the estate is green and yet ungovern'd:\nWhere every horse bears his commanding rein,\nAnd may direct his course as please himself,\nAs well the fear of harm, as harm apparent,\nIn my opinion, ought to be prevented.","sourceLine":"2.2.124","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope the king made peace with all of us\nAnd the compact is firm and true in me.","sourceLine":"2.2.132","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so in me; and so, I think, in all:\nYet, since it is but green, it should be put\nTo no apparent likelihood of breach,\nWhich haply by much company might be urged:\nTherefore I say with noble Buckingham,\nThat it is meet so few should fetch the prince.","sourceLine":"2.2.134","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:2.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so say I.","sourceLine":"2.2.140","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:2.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then be it so; and go we to determine\nWho they shall be that straight shall post to Ludlow.\nMadam, and you, my mother, will you go\nTo give your censures in this weighty business?","sourceLine":"2.2.141","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With all our harts.","sourceLine":"2.2.145","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.2:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but BUCKINGHAM and GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, whoever journeys to the Prince,\nFor God's sake, let not us two be behind;\nFor, by the way, I'll sort occasion,\nAs index to the story we late talk'd of,\nTo part the queen's proud kindred from the king.","sourceLine":"2.2.146","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:2.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My other self, my counsel's consistory,\nMy oracle, my prophet! My dear cousin,\nI, like a child, will go by thy direction.\nTowards Ludlow then, for we'll not stay behind.","sourceLine":"2.2.151","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:2.2:47"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:2.2:48"}],"scene":2},{"id":"2.3","label":"Act 2 · SCENE III. London. A street.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter two Citizens meeting","id":"richardiii:2.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Neighbour, well met: whither away so fast?","sourceLine":"2.3.1","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I promise you, I scarcely know myself:\nHear you the news abroad?","sourceLine":"2.3.2","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, that the king is dead.","sourceLine":"2.3.4","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bad news, by'r lady; seldom comes the better:\nI fear, I fear 'twill prove a troublous world.","sourceLine":"2.3.5","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Neighbours, God speed!","sourceLine":"2.3.7","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give you good morrow, sir.","sourceLine":"2.3.8","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Doth this news hold of good King Edward's death?","sourceLine":"2.3.9","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, sir, it is too true; God help the while!","sourceLine":"2.3.10","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, masters, look to see a troublous world.","sourceLine":"2.3.11","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no; by God's good grace his son shall reign.","sourceLine":"2.3.12","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Woe to the land that's govern'd by a child!","sourceLine":"2.3.13","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In him there is a hope of government,\nThat in his nonage council under him,\nAnd in his full and ripen'd years himself,\nNo doubt, shall then and till then govern well.","sourceLine":"2.3.14","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So stood the state when Henry the Sixth\nWas crown'd in Paris but at nine months old.","sourceLine":"2.3.18","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stood the state so? No, no, good friends, God wot;\nFor then this land was famously enrich'd\nWith politic grave counsel; then the king\nHad virtuous uncles to protect his grace.","sourceLine":"2.3.20","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, so hath this, both by the father and mother.","sourceLine":"2.3.24","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Better it were they all came by the father,\nOr by the father there were none at all;\nFor emulation now, who shall be nearest,\nWill touch us all too near, if God prevent not.\nO, full of danger is the Duke of Gloucester!\nAnd the queen's sons and brothers haught and proud:\nAnd were they to be ruled, and not to rule,\nThis sickly land might solace as before.","sourceLine":"2.3.25","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, we fear the worst; all shall be well.","sourceLine":"2.3.33","speaker":"First Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When clouds appear, wise men put on their cloaks;\nWhen great leaves fall, the winter is at hand;\nWhen the sun sets, who doth not look for night?\nUntimely storms make men expect a dearth.\nAll may be well; but, if God sort it so,\n'Tis more than we deserve, or I expect.","sourceLine":"2.3.34","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Truly, the souls of men are full of dread:\nYe cannot reason almost with a man\nThat looks not heavily and full of fear.","sourceLine":"2.3.40","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Before the times of change, still is it so:\nBy a divine instinct men's minds mistrust\nEnsuing dangers; as by proof, we see\nThe waters swell before a boisterous storm.\nBut leave it all to God. whither away?","sourceLine":"2.3.43","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, we were sent for to the justices.","sourceLine":"2.3.48","speaker":"Second Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so was I: I'll bear you company.","sourceLine":"2.3.49","speaker":"Third Citizen","id":"richardiii:2.3:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:2.3:25"}],"scene":3},{"id":"2.4","label":"Act 2 · SCENE IV. London. The palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the ARCHBISHOP OF YORK, young YORK, QUEEN ELIZABETH, and the DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Last night, I hear, they lay at Northampton;\nAt Stony-Stratford will they be to-night:\nTo-morrow, or next day, they will be here.","sourceLine":"2.4.1","speaker":"ARCHBISHOP OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I long with all my heart to see the prince:\nI hope he is much grown since last I saw him.","sourceLine":"2.4.4","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I hear, no; they say my son of York\nHath almost overta'en him in his growth.","sourceLine":"2.4.6","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, mother; but I would not have it so.","sourceLine":"2.4.8","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, my young cousin, it is good to grow.","sourceLine":"2.4.9","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Grandam, one night, as we did sit at supper,\nMy uncle Rivers talk'd how I did grow\nMore than my brother: 'Ay,' quoth my uncle\nGloucester,\n'Small herbs have grace, great weeds do grow apace:'\nAnd since, methinks, I would not grow so fast,\nBecause sweet flowers are slow and weeds make haste.","sourceLine":"2.4.10","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good faith, good faith, the saying did not hold\nIn him that did object the same to thee;\nHe was the wretched'st thing when he was young,\nSo long a-growing and so leisurely,\nThat, if this rule were true, he should be gracious.","sourceLine":"2.4.17","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, madam, so, no doubt, he is.","sourceLine":"2.4.22","speaker":"ARCHBISHOP OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope he is; but yet let mothers doubt.","sourceLine":"2.4.23","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by my troth, if I had been remember'd,\nI could have given my uncle's grace a flout,\nTo touch his growth nearer than he touch'd mine.","sourceLine":"2.4.24","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How, my pretty York? I pray thee, let me hear it.","sourceLine":"2.4.27","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, they say my uncle grew so fast\nThat he could gnaw a crust at two hours old\n'Twas full two years ere I could get a tooth.\nGrandam, this would have been a biting jest.","sourceLine":"2.4.28","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray thee, pretty York, who told thee this?","sourceLine":"2.4.32","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Grandam, his nurse.","sourceLine":"2.4.33","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His nurse! why, she was dead ere thou wert born.","sourceLine":"2.4.34","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If 'twere not she, I cannot tell who told me.","sourceLine":"2.4.35","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A parlous boy: go to, you are too shrewd.","sourceLine":"2.4.36","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good madam, be not angry with the child.","sourceLine":"2.4.37","speaker":"ARCHBISHOP OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pitchers have ears.","sourceLine":"2.4.38","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here comes a messenger. What news?","sourceLine":"2.4.39","speaker":"ARCHBISHOP OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such news, my lord, as grieves me to unfold.","sourceLine":"2.4.40","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How fares the prince?","sourceLine":"2.4.41","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, madam, and in health.","sourceLine":"2.4.42","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is thy news then?","sourceLine":"2.4.43","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Rivers and Lord Grey are sent to Pomfret,\nWith them Sir Thomas Vaughan, prisoners.","sourceLine":"2.4.44","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who hath committed them?","sourceLine":"2.4.46","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The mighty dukes\nGloucester and Buckingham.","sourceLine":"2.4.47","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For what offence?","sourceLine":"2.4.49","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The sum of all I can, I have disclosed;\nWhy or for what these nobles were committed\nIs all unknown to me, my gracious lady.","sourceLine":"2.4.50","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:2.4:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay me, I see the downfall of our house!\nThe tiger now hath seized the gentle hind;\nInsulting tyranny begins to jet\nUpon the innocent and aweless throne:\nWelcome, destruction, death, and massacre!\nI see, as in a map, the end of all.","sourceLine":"2.4.53","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Accursed and unquiet wrangling days,\nHow many of you have mine eyes beheld!\nMy husband lost his life to get the crown;\nAnd often up and down my sons were toss'd,\nFor me to joy and weep their gain and loss:\nAnd being seated, and domestic broils\nClean over-blown, themselves, the conquerors.\nMake war upon themselves; blood against blood,\nSelf against self: O, preposterous\nAnd frantic outrage, end thy damned spleen;\nOr let me die, to look on death no more!","sourceLine":"2.4.59","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, my boy; we will to sanctuary.\nMadam, farewell.","sourceLine":"2.4.70","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll go along with you.","sourceLine":"2.4.72","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have no cause.","sourceLine":"2.4.73","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:2.4:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious lady, go;\nAnd thither bear your treasure and your goods.\nFor my part, I'll resign unto your grace\nThe seal I keep: and so betide to me\nAs well I tender you and all of yours!\nCome, I'll conduct you to the sanctuary.","sourceLine":"2.4.74","speaker":"ARCHBISHOP OF YORK","id":"richardiii:2.4:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:2.4:38"}],"scene":4},{"id":"3.1","label":"Act 3 · SCENE I. London. A street.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"The trumpets sound. Enter the young PRINCE EDWARD, GLOUCESTER, BUCKINGHAM, CARDINAL, CATESBY, and others","id":"richardiii:3.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, sweet prince, to London, to your chamber.","sourceLine":"3.1.1","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, dear cousin, my thoughts' sovereign\nThe weary way hath made you melancholy.","sourceLine":"3.1.2","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, uncle; but our crosses on the way\nHave made it tedious, wearisome, and heavy\nI want more uncles here to welcome me.","sourceLine":"3.1.4","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet prince, the untainted virtue of your years\nHath not yet dived into the world's deceit\nNor more can you distinguish of a man\nThan of his outward show; which, God he knows,\nSeldom or never jumpeth with the heart.\nThose uncles which you want were dangerous;\nYour grace attended to their sugar'd words,\nBut look'd not on the poison of their hearts :\nGod keep you from them, and from such false friends!","sourceLine":"3.1.7","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God keep me from false friends! but they were none.","sourceLine":"3.1.16","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, the mayor of London comes to greet you.","sourceLine":"3.1.17","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Lord Mayor and his train","id":"richardiii:3.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God bless your grace with health and happy days!","sourceLine":"3.1.18","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you, good my lord; and thank you all.\nI thought my mother, and my brother York,\nWould long ere this have met us on the way\nFie, what a slug is Hastings, that he comes not\nTo tell us whether they will come or no!","sourceLine":"3.1.19","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, in good time, here comes the sweating lord.","sourceLine":"3.1.24","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, my lord: what, will our mother come?","sourceLine":"3.1.25","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"On what occasion, God he knows, not I,\nThe queen your mother, and your brother York,\nHave taken sanctuary: the tender prince\nWould fain have come with me to meet your grace,\nBut by his mother was perforce withheld.","sourceLine":"3.1.26","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie, what an indirect and peevish course\nIs this of hers! Lord cardinal, will your grace\nPersuade the queen to send the Duke of York\nUnto his princely brother presently?\nIf she deny, Lord Hastings, go with him,\nAnd from her jealous arms pluck him perforce.","sourceLine":"3.1.31","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Buckingham, if my weak oratory\nCan from his mother win the Duke of York,\nAnon expect him here; but if she be obdurate\nTo mild entreaties, God in heaven forbid\nWe should infringe the holy privilege\nOf blessed sanctuary! not for all this land\nWould I be guilty of so deep a sin.","sourceLine":"3.1.37","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"richardiii:3.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are too senseless--obstinate, my lord,\nToo ceremonious and traditional\nWeigh it but with the grossness of this age,\nYou break not sanctuary in seizing him.\nThe benefit thereof is always granted\nTo those whose dealings have deserved the place,\nAnd those who have the wit to claim the place:\nThis prince hath neither claim'd it nor deserved it;\nAnd therefore, in mine opinion, cannot have it:\nThen, taking him from thence that is not there,\nYou break no privilege nor charter there.\nOft have I heard of sanctuary men;\nBut sanctuary children ne'er till now.","sourceLine":"3.1.44","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, you shall o'er-rule my mind for once.\nCome on, Lord Hastings, will you go with me?","sourceLine":"3.1.57","speaker":"CARDINAL","id":"richardiii:3.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.1.59","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good lords, make all the speedy haste you may.","sourceLine":"3.1.60","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt CARDINAL and HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, uncle Gloucester, if our brother come,\nWhere shall we sojourn till our coronation?","sourceLine":"3.1.61","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where it seems best unto your royal self.\nIf I may counsel you, some day or two\nYour highness shall repose you at the Tower:\nThen where you please, and shall be thought most fit\nFor your best health and recreation.","sourceLine":"3.1.63","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not like the Tower, of any place.\nDid Julius Caesar build that place, my lord?","sourceLine":"3.1.68","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He did, my gracious lord, begin that place;\nWhich, since, succeeding ages have re-edified.","sourceLine":"3.1.70","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it upon record, or else reported\nSuccessively from age to age, he built it?","sourceLine":"3.1.72","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon record, my gracious lord.","sourceLine":"3.1.74","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But say, my lord, it were not register'd,\nMethinks the truth should live from age to age,\nAs 'twere retail'd to all posterity,\nEven to the general all-ending day.","sourceLine":"3.1.75","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] So wise so young, they say, do never\nlive long.","sourceLine":"3.1.79","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say you, uncle?","sourceLine":"3.1.81","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I say, without characters, fame lives long.","sourceLine":"3.1.82","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside","id":"richardiii:3.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus, like the formal vice, Iniquity,\nI moralize two meanings in one word.","sourceLine":"3.1.83","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That Julius Caesar was a famous man;\nWith what his valour did enrich his wit,\nHis wit set down to make his valour live\nDeath makes no conquest of this conqueror;\nFor now he lives in fame, though not in life.\nI'll tell you what, my cousin Buckingham,--","sourceLine":"3.1.85","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, my gracious lord?","sourceLine":"3.1.91","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An if I live until I be a man,\nI'll win our ancient right in France again,\nOr die a soldier, as I lived a king.","sourceLine":"3.1.92","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Short summers lightly have a forward spring.","sourceLine":"3.1.95","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter young YORK, HASTINGS, and the CARDINAL","id":"richardiii:3.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, in good time, here comes the Duke of York.","sourceLine":"3.1.96","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Richard of York! how fares our loving brother?","sourceLine":"3.1.97","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, my dread lord; so must I call you now.","sourceLine":"3.1.98","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, brother, to our grief, as it is yours:\nToo late he died that might have kept that title,\nWhich by his death hath lost much majesty.","sourceLine":"3.1.99","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How fares our cousin, noble Lord of York?","sourceLine":"3.1.102","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you, gentle uncle. O, my lord,\nYou said that idle weeds are fast in growth\nThe prince my brother hath outgrown me far.","sourceLine":"3.1.103","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.1.106","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And therefore is he idle?","sourceLine":"3.1.107","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my fair cousin, I must not say so.","sourceLine":"3.1.108","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then is he more beholding to you than I.","sourceLine":"3.1.109","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He may command me as my sovereign;\nBut you have power in me as in a kinsman.","sourceLine":"3.1.110","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray you, uncle, give me this dagger.","sourceLine":"3.1.112","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My dagger, little cousin? with all my heart.","sourceLine":"3.1.113","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A beggar, brother?","sourceLine":"3.1.114","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of my kind uncle, that I know will give;\nAnd being but a toy, which is no grief to give.","sourceLine":"3.1.115","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A greater gift than that I'll give my cousin.","sourceLine":"3.1.117","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A greater gift! O, that's the sword to it.","sourceLine":"3.1.118","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A gentle cousin, were it light enough.","sourceLine":"3.1.119","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, then, I see, you will part but with light gifts;\nIn weightier things you'll say a beggar nay.","sourceLine":"3.1.120","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is too heavy for your grace to wear.","sourceLine":"3.1.122","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I weigh it lightly, were it heavier.","sourceLine":"3.1.123","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, would you have my weapon, little lord?","sourceLine":"3.1.124","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would, that I might thank you as you call me.","sourceLine":"3.1.125","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How?","sourceLine":"3.1.126","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Little.","sourceLine":"3.1.127","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of York will still be cross in talk:\nUncle, your grace knows how to bear with him.","sourceLine":"3.1.128","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You mean, to bear me, not to bear with me:\nUncle, my brother mocks both you and me;\nBecause that I am little, like an ape,\nHe thinks that you should bear me on your shoulders.","sourceLine":"3.1.130","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With what a sharp-provided wit he reasons!\nTo mitigate the scorn he gives his uncle,\nHe prettily and aptly taunts himself:\nSo cunning and so young is wonderful.","sourceLine":"3.1.134","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, will't please you pass along?\nMyself and my good cousin Buckingham\nWill to your mother, to entreat of her\nTo meet you at the Tower and welcome you.","sourceLine":"3.1.138","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, will you go unto the Tower, my lord?","sourceLine":"3.1.142","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord protector needs will have it so.","sourceLine":"3.1.143","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall not sleep in quiet at the Tower.","sourceLine":"3.1.144","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, what should you fear?","sourceLine":"3.1.145","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, my uncle Clarence' angry ghost:\nMy grandam told me he was murdered there.","sourceLine":"3.1.146","speaker":"YORK","id":"richardiii:3.1:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I fear no uncles dead.","sourceLine":"3.1.148","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor none that live, I hope.","sourceLine":"3.1.149","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An if they live, I hope I need not fear.\nBut come, my lord; and with a heavy heart,\nThinking on them, go I unto the Tower.","sourceLine":"3.1.150","speaker":"PRINCE EDWARD","id":"richardiii:3.1:75"},{"kind":"stage","text":"A Sennet. Exeunt all but GLOUCESTER, BUCKINGHAM and CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Think you, my lord, this little prating York\nWas not incensed by his subtle mother\nTo taunt and scorn you thus opprobriously?","sourceLine":"3.1.153","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No doubt, no doubt; O, 'tis a parlous boy;\nBold, quick, ingenious, forward, capable\nHe is all the mother's, from the top to toe.","sourceLine":"3.1.156","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, let them rest. Come hither, Catesby.\nThou art sworn as deeply to effect what we intend\nAs closely to conceal what we impart:\nThou know'st our reasons urged upon the way;\nWhat think'st thou? is it not an easy matter\nTo make William Lord Hastings of our mind,\nFor the instalment of this noble duke\nIn the seat royal of this famous isle?","sourceLine":"3.1.159","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He for his father's sake so loves the prince,\nThat he will not be won to aught against him.","sourceLine":"3.1.167","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What think'st thou, then, of Stanley? what will he?","sourceLine":"3.1.169","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He will do all in all as Hastings doth.","sourceLine":"3.1.170","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, then, no more but this: go, gentle Catesby,\nAnd, as it were far off sound thou Lord Hastings,\nHow doth he stand affected to our purpose;\nAnd summon him to-morrow to the Tower,\nTo sit about the coronation.\nIf thou dost find him tractable to us,\nEncourage him, and show him all our reasons:\nIf he be leaden, icy-cold, unwilling,\nBe thou so too; and so break off your talk,\nAnd give us notice of his inclination:\nFor we to-morrow hold divided councils,\nWherein thyself shalt highly be employ'd.","sourceLine":"3.1.171","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Commend me to Lord William: tell him, Catesby,\nHis ancient knot of dangerous adversaries\nTo-morrow are let blood at Pomfret-castle;\nAnd bid my friend, for joy of this good news,\nGive mistress Shore one gentle kiss the more.","sourceLine":"3.1.183","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Catesby, go, effect this business soundly.","sourceLine":"3.1.188","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My good lords both, with all the heed I may.","sourceLine":"3.1.189","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall we hear from you, Catesby, ere we sleep?","sourceLine":"3.1.190","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.1.191","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At Crosby Place, there shall you find us both.","sourceLine":"3.1.192","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:89"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.1:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, my lord, what shall we do, if we perceive\nLord Hastings will not yield to our complots?","sourceLine":"3.1.193","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Chop off his head, man; somewhat we will do:\nAnd, look, when I am king, claim thou of me\nThe earldom of Hereford, and the moveables\nWhereof the king my brother stood possess'd.","sourceLine":"3.1.195","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll claim that promise at your grace's hands.","sourceLine":"3.1.199","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.1:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And look to have it yielded with all willingness.\nCome, let us sup betimes, that afterwards\nWe may digest our complots in some form.","sourceLine":"3.1.200","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.1:94"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:3.1:95"}],"scene":1},{"id":"3.2","label":"Act 3 · SCENE II. Before Lord Hastings' house.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, ho! my lord!","sourceLine":"3.2.1","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Within] Who knocks at the door?","sourceLine":"3.2.2","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A messenger from the Lord Stanley.","sourceLine":"3.2.3","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is't o'clock?","sourceLine":"3.2.4","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon the stroke of four.","sourceLine":"3.2.5","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cannot thy master sleep these tedious nights?","sourceLine":"3.2.6","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So it should seem by that I have to say.\nFirst, he commends him to your noble lordship.","sourceLine":"3.2.7","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And then?","sourceLine":"3.2.9","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And then he sends you word\nHe dreamt to-night the boar had razed his helm:\nBesides, he says there are two councils held;\nAnd that may be determined at the one\nwhich may make you and him to rue at the other.\nTherefore he sends to know your lordship's pleasure,\nIf presently you will take horse with him,\nAnd with all speed post with him toward the north,\nTo shun the danger that his soul divines.","sourceLine":"3.2.10","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, fellow, go, return unto thy lord;\nBid him not fear the separated councils\nHis honour and myself are at the one,\nAnd at the other is my servant Catesby\nWhere nothing can proceed that toucheth us\nWhereof I shall not have intelligence.\nTell him his fears are shallow, wanting instance:\nAnd for his dreams, I wonder he is so fond\nTo trust the mockery of unquiet slumbers\nTo fly the boar before the boar pursues,\nWere to incense the boar to follow us\nAnd make pursuit where he did mean no chase.\nGo, bid thy master rise and come to me\nAnd we will both together to the Tower,\nWhere, he shall see, the boar will use us kindly.","sourceLine":"3.2.19","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious lord, I'll tell him what you say.","sourceLine":"3.2.34","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:3.2:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.2:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Many good morrows to my noble lord!","sourceLine":"3.2.35","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow, Catesby; you are early stirring\nWhat news, what news, in this our tottering state?","sourceLine":"3.2.36","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is a reeling world, indeed, my lord;\nAnd I believe twill never stand upright\nTill Richard wear the garland of the realm.","sourceLine":"3.2.38","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How! wear the garland! dost thou mean the crown?","sourceLine":"3.2.41","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lord.","sourceLine":"3.2.42","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll have this crown of mine cut from my shoulders\nEre I will see the crown so foul misplaced.\nBut canst thou guess that he doth aim at it?","sourceLine":"3.2.43","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, on my life; and hopes to find forward\nUpon his party for the gain thereof:\nAnd thereupon he sends you this good news,\nThat this same very day your enemies,\nThe kindred of the queen, must die at Pomfret.","sourceLine":"3.2.46","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, I am no mourner for that news,\nBecause they have been still mine enemies:\nBut, that I'll give my voice on Richard's side,\nTo bar my master's heirs in true descent,\nGod knows I will not do it, to the death.","sourceLine":"3.2.51","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God keep your lordship in that gracious mind!","sourceLine":"3.2.56","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I shall laugh at this a twelve-month hence,\nThat they who brought me in my master's hate\nI live to look upon their tragedy.\nI tell thee, Catesby--","sourceLine":"3.2.57","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, my lord?","sourceLine":"3.2.61","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ere a fortnight make me elder,\nI'll send some packing that yet think not on it.","sourceLine":"3.2.62","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis a vile thing to die, my gracious lord,\nWhen men are unprepared and look not for it.","sourceLine":"3.2.64","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O monstrous, monstrous! and so falls it out\nWith Rivers, Vaughan, Grey: and so 'twill do\nWith some men else, who think themselves as safe\nAs thou and I; who, as thou know'st, are dear\nTo princely Richard and to Buckingham.","sourceLine":"3.2.66","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The princes both make high account of you;","sourceLine":"3.2.71","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside","id":"richardiii:3.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For they account his head upon the bridge.","sourceLine":"3.2.72","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know they do; and I have well deserved it.","sourceLine":"3.2.73","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter STANLEY","id":"richardiii:3.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come on, come on; where is your boar-spear, man?\nFear you the boar, and go so unprovided?","sourceLine":"3.2.74","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, good morrow; good morrow, Catesby:\nYou may jest on, but, by the holy rood,\nI do not like these several councils, I.","sourceLine":"3.2.76","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:3.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord,\nI hold my life as dear as you do yours;\nAnd never in my life, I do protest,\nWas it more precious to me than 'tis now:\nThink you, but that I know our state secure,\nI would be so triumphant as I am?","sourceLine":"3.2.79","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The lords at Pomfret, when they rode from London,\nWere jocund, and supposed their state was sure,\nAnd they indeed had no cause to mistrust;\nBut yet, you see how soon the day o'ercast.\nThis sudden stag of rancour I misdoubt:\nPray God, I say, I prove a needless coward!\nWhat, shall we toward the Tower? the day is spent.","sourceLine":"3.2.85","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:3.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, have with you. Wot you what, my lord?\nTo-day the lords you talk of are beheaded.","sourceLine":"3.2.92","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They, for their truth, might better wear their heads\nThan some that have accused them wear their hats.\nBut come, my lord, let us away.","sourceLine":"3.2.94","speaker":"LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:3.2:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Pursuivant","id":"richardiii:3.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go on before; I'll talk with this good fellow.","sourceLine":"3.2.97","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt STANLEY and CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, sirrah! how goes the world with thee?","sourceLine":"3.2.98","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The better that your lordship please to ask.","sourceLine":"3.2.99","speaker":"Pursuivant","id":"richardiii:3.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I tell thee, man, 'tis better with me now\nThan when I met thee last where now we meet:\nThen was I going prisoner to the Tower,\nBy the suggestion of the queen's allies;\nBut now, I tell thee--keep it to thyself--\nThis day those enemies are put to death,\nAnd I in better state than e'er I was.","sourceLine":"3.2.100","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God hold it, to your honour's good content!","sourceLine":"3.2.107","speaker":"Pursuivant","id":"richardiii:3.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gramercy, fellow: there, drink that for me.","sourceLine":"3.2.108","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Throws him his purse","id":"richardiii:3.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God save your lordship!","sourceLine":"3.2.109","speaker":"Pursuivant","id":"richardiii:3.2:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.2:51"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Priest","id":"richardiii:3.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well met, my lord; I am glad to see your honour.","sourceLine":"3.2.110","speaker":"Priest","id":"richardiii:3.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank thee, good Sir John, with all my heart.\nI am in your debt for your last exercise;\nCome the next Sabbath, and I will content you.","sourceLine":"3.2.111","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:54"},{"kind":"stage","text":"He whispers in his ear","id":"richardiii:3.2:55"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, talking with a priest, lord chamberlain?\nYour friends at Pomfret, they do need the priest;\nYour honour hath no shriving work in hand.","sourceLine":"3.2.114","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good faith, and when I met this holy man,\nThose men you talk of came into my mind.\nWhat, go you toward the Tower?","sourceLine":"3.2.117","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do, my lord; but long I shall not stay\nI shall return before your lordship thence.","sourceLine":"3.2.120","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis like enough, for I stay dinner there.","sourceLine":"3.2.122","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] And supper too, although thou know'st it not.\nCome, will you go?","sourceLine":"3.2.123","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll wait upon your lordship.","sourceLine":"3.2.125","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.2:62"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:3.2:63"}],"scene":2},{"id":"3.3","label":"Act 3 · SCENE III. Pomfret Castle.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter RATCLIFF, with halberds, carrying RIVERS, GREY, and VAUGHAN to death","id":"richardiii:3.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, bring forth the prisoners.","sourceLine":"3.3.1","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:3.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir Richard Ratcliff, let me tell thee this:\nTo-day shalt thou behold a subject die\nFor truth, for duty, and for loyalty.","sourceLine":"3.3.2","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:3.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God keep the prince from all the pack of you!\nA knot you are of damned blood-suckers!","sourceLine":"3.3.5","speaker":"GREY","id":"richardiii:3.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You live that shall cry woe for this after.","sourceLine":"3.3.7","speaker":"VAUGHAN","id":"richardiii:3.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dispatch; the limit of your lives is out.","sourceLine":"3.3.8","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:3.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Pomfret, Pomfret! O thou bloody prison,\nFatal and ominous to noble peers!\nWithin the guilty closure of thy walls\nRichard the second here was hack'd to death;\nAnd, for more slander to thy dismal seat,\nWe give thee up our guiltless blood to drink.","sourceLine":"3.3.9","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:3.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now Margaret's curse is fall'n upon our heads,\nFor standing by when Richard stabb'd her son.","sourceLine":"3.3.15","speaker":"GREY","id":"richardiii:3.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then cursed she Hastings, then cursed she Buckingham,\nThen cursed she Richard. O, remember, God\nTo hear her prayers for them, as now for us\nAnd for my sister and her princely sons,\nBe satisfied, dear God, with our true blood,\nWhich, as thou know'st, unjustly must be spilt.","sourceLine":"3.3.17","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:3.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make haste; the hour of death is expiate.","sourceLine":"3.3.23","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:3.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, Grey, come, Vaughan, let us all embrace:\nAnd take our leave, until we meet in heaven.","sourceLine":"3.3.24","speaker":"RIVERS","id":"richardiii:3.3:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:3.3:12"}],"scene":3},{"id":"3.4","label":"Act 3 · SCENE IV. The Tower of London.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BUCKINGHAM, DERBY, HASTINGS, the BISHOP OF ELY, RATCLIFF, LOVEL, with others, and take their seats at a table","id":"richardiii:3.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lords, at once: the cause why we are met\nIs, to determine of the coronation.\nIn God's name, speak: when is the royal day?","sourceLine":"3.4.1","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are all things fitting for that royal time?","sourceLine":"3.4.4","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is, and wants but nomination.","sourceLine":"3.4.5","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:3.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To-morrow, then, I judge a happy day.","sourceLine":"3.4.6","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who knows the lord protector's mind herein?\nWho is most inward with the royal duke?","sourceLine":"3.4.7","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your grace, we think, should soonest know his mind.","sourceLine":"3.4.9","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who, I, my lord I we know each other's faces,\nBut for our hearts, he knows no more of mine,\nThan I of yours;\nNor I no more of his, than you of mine.\nLord Hastings, you and he are near in love.","sourceLine":"3.4.10","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank his grace, I know he loves me well;\nBut, for his purpose in the coronation.\nI have not sounded him, nor he deliver'd\nHis gracious pleasure any way therein:\nBut you, my noble lords, may name the time;\nAnd in the duke's behalf I'll give my voice,\nWhich, I presume, he'll take in gentle part.","sourceLine":"3.4.15","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now in good time, here comes the duke himself.","sourceLine":"3.4.22","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My noble lords and cousins all, good morrow.\nI have been long a sleeper; but, I hope,\nMy absence doth neglect no great designs,\nWhich by my presence might have been concluded.","sourceLine":"3.4.23","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Had not you come upon your cue, my lord\nWilliam Lord Hastings had pronounced your part,--\nI mean, your voice,--for crowning of the king.","sourceLine":"3.4.27","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Than my Lord Hastings no man might be bolder;\nHis lordship knows me well, and loves me well.","sourceLine":"3.4.30","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank your grace.","sourceLine":"3.4.32","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord of Ely!","sourceLine":"3.4.33","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"3.4.34","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When I was last in Holborn,\nI saw good strawberries in your garden there\nI do beseech you send for some of them.","sourceLine":"3.4.35","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, and will, my lord, with all my heart.","sourceLine":"3.4.38","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousin of Buckingham, a word with you.","sourceLine":"3.4.39","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drawing him aside","id":"richardiii:3.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Catesby hath sounded Hastings in our business,\nAnd finds the testy gentleman so hot,\nAs he will lose his head ere give consent\nHis master's son, as worshipful as he terms it,\nShall lose the royalty of England's throne.","sourceLine":"3.4.40","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Withdraw you hence, my lord, I'll follow you.","sourceLine":"3.4.45","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit GLOUCESTER, BUCKINGHAM following","id":"richardiii:3.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have not yet set down this day of triumph.\nTo-morrow, in mine opinion, is too sudden;\nFor I myself am not so well provided\nAs else I would be, were the day prolong'd.","sourceLine":"3.4.46","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:3.4:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is my lord protector? I have sent for these\nstrawberries.","sourceLine":"3.4.50","speaker":"BISHOP OF ELY","id":"richardiii:3.4:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His grace looks cheerfully and smooth to-day;\nThere's some conceit or other likes him well,\nWhen he doth bid good morrow with such a spirit.\nI think there's never a man in Christendom\nThat can less hide his love or hate than he;\nFor by his face straight shall you know his heart.","sourceLine":"3.4.52","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What of his heart perceive you in his face\nBy any likelihood he show'd to-day?","sourceLine":"3.4.58","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:3.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, that with no man here he is offended;\nFor, were he, he had shown it in his looks.","sourceLine":"3.4.60","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray God he be not, I say.","sourceLine":"3.4.62","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:3.4:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter GLOUCESTER and BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray you all, tell me what they deserve\nThat do conspire my death with devilish plots\nOf damned witchcraft, and that have prevail'd\nUpon my body with their hellish charms?","sourceLine":"3.4.63","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The tender love I bear your grace, my lord,\nMakes me most forward in this noble presence\nTo doom the offenders, whatsoever they be\nI say, my lord, they have deserved death.","sourceLine":"3.4.67","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then be your eyes the witness of this ill:\nSee how I am bewitch'd; behold mine arm\nIs, like a blasted sapling, wither'd up:\nAnd this is Edward's wife, that monstrous witch,\nConsorted with that harlot strumpet Shore,\nThat by their witchcraft thus have marked me.","sourceLine":"3.4.71","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If they have done this thing, my gracious lord--","sourceLine":"3.4.77","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I thou protector of this damned strumpet--\nTellest thou me of 'ifs'? Thou art a traitor:\nOff with his head! Now, by Saint Paul I swear,\nI will not dine until I see the same.\nLovel and Ratcliff, look that it be done:\nThe rest, that love me, rise and follow me.","sourceLine":"3.4.78","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.4:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but HASTINGS, RATCLIFF, and LOVEL","id":"richardiii:3.4:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Woe, woe for England! not a whit for me;\nFor I, too fond, might have prevented this.\nStanley did dream the boar did raze his helm;\nBut I disdain'd it, and did scorn to fly:\nThree times to-day my foot-cloth horse did stumble,\nAnd startled, when he look'd upon the Tower,\nAs loath to bear me to the slaughter-house.\nO, now I want the priest that spake to me:\nI now repent I told the pursuivant\nAs 'twere triumphing at mine enemies,\nHow they at Pomfret bloodily were butcher'd,\nAnd I myself secure in grace and favour.\nO Margaret, Margaret, now thy heavy curse\nIs lighted on poor Hastings' wretched head!","sourceLine":"3.4.84","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dispatch, my lord; the duke would be at dinner:\nMake a short shrift; he longs to see your head.","sourceLine":"3.4.98","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:3.4:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O momentary grace of mortal men,\nWhich we more hunt for than the grace of God!\nWho builds his hopes in air of your good looks,\nLives like a drunken sailor on a mast,\nReady, with every nod, to tumble down\nInto the fatal bowels of the deep.","sourceLine":"3.4.100","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, dispatch; 'tis bootless to exclaim.","sourceLine":"3.4.106","speaker":"LOVEL","id":"richardiii:3.4:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O bloody Richard! miserable England!\nI prophesy the fearful'st time to thee\nThat ever wretched age hath look'd upon.\nCome, lead me to the block; bear him my head.\nThey smile at me that shortly shall be dead.","sourceLine":"3.4.107","speaker":"HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:3.4:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:3.4:45"}],"scene":4},{"id":"3.5","label":"Act 3 · SCENE V. The Tower-walls.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER and BUCKINGHAM, in rotten armour, marvellous ill-favoured","id":"richardiii:3.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, cousin, canst thou quake, and change thy colour,\nMurder thy breath in the middle of a word,\nAnd then begin again, and stop again,\nAs if thou wert distraught and mad with terror?","sourceLine":"3.5.1","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, I can counterfeit the deep tragedian;\nSpeak and look back, and pry on every side,\nTremble and start at wagging of a straw,\nIntending deep suspicion: ghastly looks\nAre at my service, like enforced smiles;\nAnd both are ready in their offices,\nAt any time, to grace my stratagems.\nBut what, is Catesby gone?","sourceLine":"3.5.5","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is; and, see, he brings the mayor along.","sourceLine":"3.5.13","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Lord Mayor and CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord mayor,--","sourceLine":"3.5.14","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look to the drawbridge there!","sourceLine":"3.5.15","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark! a drum.","sourceLine":"3.5.16","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Catesby, o'erlook the walls.","sourceLine":"3.5.17","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord mayor, the reason we have sent--","sourceLine":"3.5.18","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look back, defend thee, here are enemies.","sourceLine":"3.5.19","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God and our innocency defend and guard us!","sourceLine":"3.5.20","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be patient, they are friends, Ratcliff and Lovel.","sourceLine":"3.5.21","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LOVEL and RATCLIFF, with HASTINGS' head","id":"richardiii:3.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here is the head of that ignoble traitor,\nThe dangerous and unsuspected Hastings.","sourceLine":"3.5.22","speaker":"LOVEL","id":"richardiii:3.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So dear I loved the man, that I must weep.\nI took him for the plainest harmless creature\nThat breathed upon this earth a Christian;\nMade him my book wherein my soul recorded\nThe history of all her secret thoughts:\nSo smooth he daub'd his vice with show of virtue,\nThat, his apparent open guilt omitted,\nI mean, his conversation with Shore's wife,\nHe lived from all attainder of suspect.","sourceLine":"3.5.24","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, well, he was the covert'st shelter'd traitor\nThat ever lived.\nWould you imagine, or almost believe,\nWere't not that, by great preservation,\nWe live to tell it you, the subtle traitor\nThis day had plotted, in the council-house\nTo murder me and my good Lord of Gloucester?","sourceLine":"3.5.33","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, had he so?","sourceLine":"3.5.40","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, think You we are Turks or infidels?\nOr that we would, against the form of law,\nProceed thus rashly to the villain's death,\nBut that the extreme peril of the case,\nThe peace of England and our persons' safety,\nEnforced us to this execution?","sourceLine":"3.5.41","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, fair befall you! he deserved his death;\nAnd you my good lords, both have well proceeded,\nTo warn false traitors from the like attempts.\nI never look'd for better at his hands,\nAfter he once fell in with Mistress Shore.","sourceLine":"3.5.47","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet had not we determined he should die,\nUntil your lordship came to see his death;\nWhich now the loving haste of these our friends,\nSomewhat against our meaning, have prevented:\nBecause, my lord, we would have had you heard\nThe traitor speak, and timorously confess\nThe manner and the purpose of his treason;\nThat you might well have signified the same\nUnto the citizens, who haply may\nMisconstrue us in him and wail his death.","sourceLine":"3.5.52","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But, my good lord, your grace's word shall serve,\nAs well as I had seen and heard him speak\nAnd doubt you not, right noble princes both,\nBut I'll acquaint our duteous citizens\nWith all your just proceedings in this cause.","sourceLine":"3.5.62","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And to that end we wish'd your lord-ship here,\nTo avoid the carping censures of the world.","sourceLine":"3.5.67","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But since you come too late of our intents,\nYet witness what you hear we did intend:\nAnd so, my good lord mayor, we bid farewell.","sourceLine":"3.5.69","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, after, after, cousin Buckingham.\nThe mayor towards Guildhall hies him in all post:\nThere, at your meet'st advantage of the time,\nInfer the bastardy of Edward's children:\nTell them how Edward put to death a citizen,\nOnly for saying he would make his son\nHeir to the crown; meaning indeed his house,\nWhich, by the sign thereof was termed so.\nMoreover, urge his hateful luxury\nAnd bestial appetite in change of lust;\nWhich stretched to their servants, daughters, wives,\nEven where his lustful eye or savage heart,\nWithout control, listed to make his prey.\nNay, for a need, thus far come near my person:\nTell them, when that my mother went with child\nOf that unsatiate Edward, noble York\nMy princely father then had wars in France\nAnd, by just computation of the time,\nFound that the issue was not his begot;\nWhich well appeared in his lineaments,\nBeing nothing like the noble duke my father:\nBut touch this sparingly, as 'twere far off,\nBecause you know, my lord, my mother lives.","sourceLine":"3.5.72","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fear not, my lord, I'll play the orator\nAs if the golden fee for which I plead\nWere for myself: and so, my lord, adieu.","sourceLine":"3.5.95","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you thrive well, bring them to Baynard's Castle;\nWhere you shall find me well accompanied\nWith reverend fathers and well-learned bishops.","sourceLine":"3.5.98","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go: and towards three or four o'clock\nLook for the news that the Guildhall affords.","sourceLine":"3.5.101","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, Lovel, with all speed to Doctor Shaw;","sourceLine":"3.5.103","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go thou to Friar Penker; bid them both\nMeet me within this hour at Baynard's Castle.","sourceLine":"3.5.104","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now will I in, to take some privy order,\nTo draw the brats of Clarence out of sight;\nAnd to give notice, that no manner of person\nAt any time have recourse unto the princes.","sourceLine":"3.5.106","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.5:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.5:36"}],"scene":5},{"id":"3.6","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VI. The same.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Scrivener, with a paper in his hand","id":"richardiii:3.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the indictment of the good Lord Hastings;\nWhich in a set hand fairly is engross'd,\nThat it may be this day read over in Paul's.\nAnd mark how well the sequel hangs together:\nEleven hours I spent to write it over,\nFor yesternight by Catesby was it brought me;\nThe precedent was full as long a-doing:\nAnd yet within these five hours lived Lord Hastings,\nUntainted, unexamined, free, at liberty\nHere's a good world the while! Why who's so gross,\nThat seeth not this palpable device?\nYet who's so blind, but says he sees it not?\nBad is the world; and all will come to nought,\nWhen such bad dealings must be seen in thought.","sourceLine":"3.6.1","speaker":"Scrivener","id":"richardiii:3.6:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.6:3"}],"scene":6},{"id":"3.7","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VII. Baynard's Castle.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER and BUCKINGHAM, at several doors","id":"richardiii:3.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, my lord, what say the citizens?","sourceLine":"3.7.1","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by the holy mother of our Lord,\nThe citizens are mum and speak not a word.","sourceLine":"3.7.2","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Touch'd you the bastardy of Edward's children?","sourceLine":"3.7.4","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did; with his contract with Lady Lucy,\nAnd his contract by deputy in France;\nThe insatiate greediness of his desires,\nAnd his enforcement of the city wives;\nHis tyranny for trifles; his own bastardy,\nAs being got, your father then in France,\nHis resemblance, being not like the duke;\nWithal I did infer your lineaments,\nBeing the right idea of your father,\nBoth in your form and nobleness of mind;\nLaid open all your victories in Scotland,\nYour dicipline in war, wisdom in peace,\nYour bounty, virtue, fair humility:\nIndeed, left nothing fitting for the purpose\nUntouch'd, or slightly handled, in discourse\nAnd when mine oratory grew to an end\nI bid them that did love their country's good\nCry 'God save Richard, England's royal king!'","sourceLine":"3.7.5","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah! and did they so?","sourceLine":"3.7.23","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, so God help me, they spake not a word;\nBut, like dumb statues or breathing stones,\nGazed each on other, and look'd deadly pale.\nWhich when I saw, I reprehended them;\nAnd ask'd the mayor what meant this wilful silence:\nHis answer was, the people were not wont\nTo be spoke to but by the recorder.\nThen he was urged to tell my tale again,\n'Thus saith the duke, thus hath the duke inferr'd;'\nBut nothing spake in warrant from himself.\nWhen he had done, some followers of mine own,\nAt the lower end of the hall, hurl'd up their caps,\nAnd some ten voices cried 'God save King Richard!'\nAnd thus I took the vantage of those few,\n'Thanks, gentle citizens and friends,' quoth I;\n'This general applause and loving shout\nArgues your wisdoms and your love to Richard:'\nAnd even here brake off, and came away.","sourceLine":"3.7.24","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What tongueless blocks were they! would not they speak?","sourceLine":"3.7.42","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, by my troth, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.7.43","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will not the mayor then and his brethren come?","sourceLine":"3.7.44","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The mayor is here at hand: intend some fear;\nBe not you spoke with, but by mighty suit:\nAnd look you get a prayer-book in your hand,\nAnd stand betwixt two churchmen, good my lord;\nFor on that ground I'll build a holy descant:\nAnd be not easily won to our request:\nPlay the maid's part, still answer nay, and take it.","sourceLine":"3.7.45","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go; and if you plead as well for them\nAs I can say nay to thee for myself,\nNo doubt well bring it to a happy issue.","sourceLine":"3.7.52","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, go, up to the leads; the lord mayor knocks.","sourceLine":"3.7.55","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Lord Mayor and Citizens","id":"richardiii:3.7:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome my lord; I dance attendance here;\nI think the duke will not be spoke withal.","sourceLine":"3.7.56","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here comes his servant: how now, Catesby,\nWhat says he?","sourceLine":"3.7.58","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord: he doth entreat your grace;\nTo visit him to-morrow or next day:\nHe is within, with two right reverend fathers,\nDivinely bent to meditation;\nAnd no worldly suit would he be moved,\nTo draw him from his holy exercise.","sourceLine":"3.7.60","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Return, good Catesby, to thy lord again;\nTell him, myself, the mayor and citizens,\nIn deep designs and matters of great moment,\nNo less importing than our general good,\nAre come to have some conference with his grace.","sourceLine":"3.7.66","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll tell him what you say, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.7.71","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:3.7:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, ha, my lord, this prince is not an Edward!\nHe is not lolling on a lewd day-bed,\nBut on his knees at meditation;\nNot dallying with a brace of courtezans,\nBut meditating with two deep divines;\nNot sleeping, to engross his idle body,\nBut praying, to enrich his watchful soul:\nHappy were England, would this gracious prince\nTake on himself the sovereignty thereof:\nBut, sure, I fear, we shall ne'er win him to it.","sourceLine":"3.7.72","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, God forbid his grace should say us nay!","sourceLine":"3.7.82","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.7:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I fear he will.","sourceLine":"3.7.83","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, Catesby, what says your lord?","sourceLine":"3.7.84","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord,\nHe wonders to what end you have assembled\nSuch troops of citizens to speak with him,\nHis grace not being warn'd thereof before:\nMy lord, he fears you mean no good to him.","sourceLine":"3.7.85","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sorry I am my noble cousin should\nSuspect me, that I mean no good to him:\nBy heaven, I come in perfect love to him;\nAnd so once more return and tell his grace.","sourceLine":"3.7.90","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When holy and devout religious men\nAre at their beads, 'tis hard to draw them thence,\nSo sweet is zealous contemplation.","sourceLine":"3.7.94","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter GLOUCESTER aloft, between two Bishops. CATESBY returns","id":"richardiii:3.7:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See, where he stands between two clergymen!","sourceLine":"3.7.97","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.7:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Two props of virtue for a Christian prince,\nTo stay him from the fall of vanity:\nAnd, see, a book of prayer in his hand,\nTrue ornaments to know a holy man.\nFamous Plantagenet, most gracious prince,\nLend favourable ears to our request;\nAnd pardon us the interruption\nOf thy devotion and right Christian zeal.","sourceLine":"3.7.98","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, there needs no such apology:\nI rather do beseech you pardon me,\nWho, earnest in the service of my God,\nNeglect the visitation of my friends.\nBut, leaving this, what is your grace's pleasure?","sourceLine":"3.7.106","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even that, I hope, which pleaseth God above,\nAnd all good men of this ungovern'd isle.","sourceLine":"3.7.111","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do suspect I have done some offence\nThat seems disgracious in the city's eyes,\nAnd that you come to reprehend my ignorance.","sourceLine":"3.7.113","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have, my lord: would it might please your grace,\nAt our entreaties, to amend that fault!","sourceLine":"3.7.116","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Else wherefore breathe I in a Christian land?","sourceLine":"3.7.118","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then know, it is your fault that you resign\nThe supreme seat, the throne majestical,\nThe scepter'd office of your ancestors,\nYour state of fortune and your due of birth,\nThe lineal glory of your royal house,\nTo the corruption of a blemished stock:\nWhilst, in the mildness of your sleepy thoughts,\nWhich here we waken to our country's good,\nThis noble isle doth want her proper limbs;\nHer face defaced with scars of infamy,\nHer royal stock graft with ignoble plants,\nAnd almost shoulder'd in the swallowing gulf\nOf blind forgetfulness and dark oblivion.\nWhich to recure, we heartily solicit\nYour gracious self to take on you the charge\nAnd kingly government of this your land,\nNot as protector, steward, substitute,\nOr lowly factor for another's gain;\nBut as successively from blood to blood,\nYour right of birth, your empery, your own.\nFor this, consorted with the citizens,\nYour very worshipful and loving friends,\nAnd by their vehement instigation,\nIn this just suit come I to move your grace.","sourceLine":"3.7.119","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not whether to depart in silence,\nOr bitterly to speak in your reproof.\nBest fitteth my degree or your condition\nIf not to answer, you might haply think\nTongue-tied ambition, not replying, yielded\nTo bear the golden yoke of sovereignty,\nWhich fondly you would here impose on me;\nIf to reprove you for this suit of yours,\nSo season'd with your faithful love to me.\nThen, on the other side, I cheque'd my friends.\nTherefore, to speak, and to avoid the first,\nAnd then, in speaking, not to incur the last,\nDefinitively thus I answer you.\nYour love deserves my thanks; but my desert\nUnmeritable shuns your high request.\nFirst if all obstacles were cut away,\nAnd that my path were even to the crown,\nAs my ripe revenue and due by birth\nYet so much is my poverty of spirit,\nSo mighty and so many my defects,\nAs I had rather hide me from my greatness,\nBeing a bark to brook no mighty sea,\nThan in my greatness covet to be hid,\nAnd in the vapour of my glory smother'd.\nBut, God be thank'd, there's no need of me,\nAnd much I need to help you, if need were;\nThe royal tree hath left us royal fruit,\nWhich, mellow'd by the stealing hours of time,\nWill well become the seat of majesty,\nAnd make, no doubt, us happy by his reign.\nOn him I lay what you would lay on me,\nThe right and fortune of his happy stars;\nWhich God defend that I should wring from him!","sourceLine":"3.7.143","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, this argues conscience in your grace;\nBut the respects thereof are nice and trivial,\nAll circumstances well considered.\nYou say that Edward is your brother's son:\nSo say we too, but not by Edward's wife;\nFor first he was contract to Lady Lucy--\nYour mother lives a witness to that vow--\nAnd afterward by substitute betroth'd\nTo Bona, sister to the King of France.\nThese both put by a poor petitioner,\nA care-crazed mother of a many children,\nA beauty-waning and distressed widow,\nEven in the afternoon of her best days,\nMade prize and purchase of his lustful eye,\nSeduced the pitch and height of all his thoughts\nTo base declension and loathed bigamy\nBy her, in his unlawful bed, he got\nThis Edward, whom our manners term the prince.\nMore bitterly could I expostulate,\nSave that, for reverence to some alive,\nI give a sparing limit to my tongue.\nThen, good my lord, take to your royal self\nThis proffer'd benefit of dignity;\nIf non to bless us and the land withal,\nYet to draw forth your noble ancestry\nFrom the corruption of abusing times,\nUnto a lineal true-derived course.","sourceLine":"3.7.176","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, good my lord, your citizens entreat you.","sourceLine":"3.7.203","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.7:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Refuse not, mighty lord, this proffer'd love.","sourceLine":"3.7.204","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, make them joyful, grant their lawful suit!","sourceLine":"3.7.205","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, why would you heap these cares on me?\nI am unfit for state and majesty;\nI do beseech you, take it not amiss;\nI cannot nor I will not yield to you.","sourceLine":"3.7.206","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you refuse it,--as, in love and zeal,\nLoath to depose the child, Your brother's son;\nAs well we know your tenderness of heart\nAnd gentle, kind, effeminate remorse,\nWhich we have noted in you to your kin,\nAnd egally indeed to all estates,--\nYet whether you accept our suit or no,\nYour brother's son shall never reign our king;\nBut we will plant some other in the throne,\nTo the disgrace and downfall of your house:\nAnd in this resolution here we leave you.--\nCome, citizens: 'zounds! I'll entreat no more.","sourceLine":"3.7.210","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, do not swear, my lord of Buckingham.","sourceLine":"3.7.222","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit BUCKINGHAM with the Citizens","id":"richardiii:3.7:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Call them again, my lord, and accept their suit.","sourceLine":"3.7.223","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:3.7:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, good my lord, lest all the land do rue it.","sourceLine":"3.7.224","speaker":"ANOTHER","id":"richardiii:3.7:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would you enforce me to a world of care?\nWell, call them again. I am not made of stone,\nBut penetrable to your. kind entreats,\nAlbeit against my conscience and my soul.","sourceLine":"3.7.225","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:52"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter BUCKINGHAM and the rest","id":"richardiii:3.7:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousin of Buckingham, and you sage, grave men,\nSince you will buckle fortune on my back,\nTo bear her burthen, whether I will or no,\nI must have patience to endure the load:\nBut if black scandal or foul-faced reproach\nAttend the sequel of your imposition,\nYour mere enforcement shall acquittance me\nFrom all the impure blots and stains thereof;\nFor God he knows, and you may partly see,\nHow far I am from the desire thereof.","sourceLine":"3.7.229","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God bless your grace! we see it, and will say it.","sourceLine":"3.7.239","speaker":"Lord Mayor","id":"richardiii:3.7:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In saying so, you shall but say the truth.","sourceLine":"3.7.240","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then I salute you with this kingly title:\nLong live Richard, England's royal king!","sourceLine":"3.7.241","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Amen.","sourceLine":"3.7.243","speaker":"Citizens","id":"richardiii:3.7:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To-morrow will it please you to be crown'd?","sourceLine":"3.7.244","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even when you please, since you will have it so.","sourceLine":"3.7.245","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To-morrow, then, we will attend your grace:\nAnd so most joyfully we take our leave.","sourceLine":"3.7.246","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:3.7:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, let us to our holy task again.\nFarewell, good cousin; farewell, gentle friends.","sourceLine":"3.7.248","speaker":"GLOUCESTER","id":"richardiii:3.7:62"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:3.7:63"}],"scene":7},{"id":"4.1","label":"Act 4 · SCENE I. Before the Tower.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, on one side, QUEEN ELIZABETH, DUCHESS OF YORK, and DORSET; on the other, ANNE, Duchess of Gloucester, leading Lady Margaret Plantagenet, CLARENCE's young Daughter","id":"richardiii:4.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who m eets us here? my niece Plantagenet\nLed in the hand of her kind aunt of Gloucester?\nNow, for my life, she's wandering to the Tower,\nOn pure heart's love to greet the tender princes.\nDaughter, well met.","sourceLine":"4.1.1","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God give your graces both\nA happy and a joyful time of day!","sourceLine":"4.1.6","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As much to you, good sister! Whither away?","sourceLine":"4.1.8","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No farther than the Tower; and, as I guess,\nUpon the like devotion as yourselves,\nTo gratulate the gentle princes there.","sourceLine":"4.1.9","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Kind sister, thanks: we'll enter all together.","sourceLine":"4.1.12","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:4.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And, in good time, here the lieutenant comes.\nMaster lieutenant, pray you, by your leave,\nHow doth the prince, and my young son of York?","sourceLine":"4.1.13","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Right well, dear madam. By your patience,\nI may not suffer you to visit them;\nThe king hath straitly charged the contrary.","sourceLine":"4.1.16","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:4.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The king! why, who's that?","sourceLine":"4.1.19","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cry you mercy: I mean the lord protector.","sourceLine":"4.1.20","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:4.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Lord protect him from that kingly title!\nHath he set bounds betwixt their love and me?\nI am their mother; who should keep me from them?","sourceLine":"4.1.21","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am their fathers mother; I will see them.","sourceLine":"4.1.24","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Their aunt I am in law, in love their mother:\nThen bring me to their sights; I'll bear thy blame\nAnd take thy office from thee, on my peril.","sourceLine":"4.1.25","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, madam, no; I may not leave it so:\nI am bound by oath, and therefore pardon me.","sourceLine":"4.1.28","speaker":"BRAKENBURY","id":"richardiii:4.1:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me but meet you, ladies, one hour hence,\nAnd I'll salute your grace of York as mother,\nAnd reverend looker on, of two fair queens.","sourceLine":"4.1.30","speaker":"LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.1:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, madam, you must straight to Westminster,\nThere to be crowned Richard's royal queen.","sourceLine":"4.1.33","speaker":"LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, cut my lace in sunder, that my pent heart\nMay have some scope to beat, or else I swoon\nWith this dead-killing news!","sourceLine":"4.1.35","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Despiteful tidings! O unpleasing news!","sourceLine":"4.1.38","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be of good cheer: mother, how fares your grace?","sourceLine":"4.1.39","speaker":"DORSET","id":"richardiii:4.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Dorset, speak not to me, get thee hence!\nDeath and destruction dog thee at the heels;\nThy mother's name is ominous to children.\nIf thou wilt outstrip death, go cross the seas,\nAnd live with Richmond, from the reach of hell\nGo, hie thee, hie thee from this slaughter-house,\nLest thou increase the number of the dead;\nAnd make me die the thrall of Margaret's curse,\nNor mother, wife, nor England's counted queen.","sourceLine":"4.1.40","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Full of wise care is this your counsel, madam.\nTake all the swift advantage of the hours;\nYou shall have letters from me to my son\nTo meet you on the way, and welcome you.\nBe not ta'en tardy by unwise delay.","sourceLine":"4.1.49","speaker":"LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O ill-dispersing wind of misery!\nO my accursed womb, the bed of death!\nA cockatrice hast thou hatch'd to the world,\nWhose unavoided eye is murderous.","sourceLine":"4.1.54","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, madam, come; I in all haste was sent.","sourceLine":"4.1.58","speaker":"LORD STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I in all unwillingness will go.\nI would to God that the inclusive verge\nOf golden metal that must round my brow\nWere red-hot steel, to sear me to the brain!\nAnointed let me be with deadly venom,\nAnd die, ere men can say, God save the queen!","sourceLine":"4.1.59","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, go, poor soul, I envy not thy glory\nTo feed my humour, wish thyself no harm.","sourceLine":"4.1.65","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No! why? When he that is my husband now\nCame to me, as I follow'd Henry's corse,\nWhen scarce the blood was well wash'd from his hands\nWhich issued from my other angel husband\nAnd that dead saint which then I weeping follow'd;\nO, when, I say, I look'd on Richard's face,\nThis was my wish: 'Be thou,' quoth I, ' accursed,\nFor making me, so young, so old a widow!\nAnd, when thou wed'st, let sorrow haunt thy bed;\nAnd be thy wife--if any be so mad--\nAs miserable by the life of thee\nAs thou hast made me by my dear lord's death!\nLo, ere I can repeat this curse again,\nEven in so short a space, my woman's heart\nGrossly grew captive to his honey words\nAnd proved the subject of my own soul's curse,\nWhich ever since hath kept my eyes from rest;\nFor never yet one hour in his bed\nHave I enjoy'd the golden dew of sleep,\nBut have been waked by his timorous dreams.\nBesides, he hates me for my father Warwick;\nAnd will, no doubt, shortly be rid of me.","sourceLine":"4.1.67","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor heart, adieu! I pity thy complaining.","sourceLine":"4.1.89","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more than from my soul I mourn for yours.","sourceLine":"4.1.90","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, thou woful welcomer of glory!","sourceLine":"4.1.91","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Adieu, poor soul, that takest thy leave of it!","sourceLine":"4.1.92","speaker":"LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To DORSET]\nGo thou to Richmond, and good fortune guide thee!","sourceLine":"4.1.93","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:4.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go thou to Richard, and good angels guard thee!","sourceLine":"4.1.95","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go thou to sanctuary, and good thoughts possess thee!\nI to my grave, where peace and rest lie with me!\nEighty odd years of sorrow have I seen,\nAnd each hour's joy wrecked with a week of teen.","sourceLine":"4.1.96","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, yet look back with me unto the Tower.\nPity, you ancient stones, those tender babes\nWhom envy hath immured within your walls!\nRough cradle for such little pretty ones!\nRude ragged nurse, old sullen playfellow\nFor tender princes, use my babies well!\nSo foolish sorrow bids your stones farewell.","sourceLine":"4.1.100","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.1:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:4.1:41"}],"scene":1},{"id":"4.2","label":"Act 4 · SCENE II. London. The palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Sennet. Enter KING RICHARD III, in pomp, crowned; BUCKINGHAM, CATESBY, a page, and others","id":"richardiii:4.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand all apart Cousin of Buckingham!","sourceLine":"4.2.1","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious sovereign?","sourceLine":"4.2.2","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me thy hand.","sourceLine":"4.2.3","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Here he ascendeth his throne","id":"richardiii:4.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus high, by thy advice\nAnd thy assistance, is King Richard seated;\nBut shall we wear these honours for a day?\nOr shall they last, and we rejoice in them?","sourceLine":"4.2.4","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Still live they and for ever may they last!","sourceLine":"4.2.8","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Buckingham, now do I play the touch,\nTo try if thou be current gold indeed\nYoung Edward lives: think now what I would say.","sourceLine":"4.2.9","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say on, my loving lord.","sourceLine":"4.2.12","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Buckingham, I say, I would be king,","sourceLine":"4.2.13","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, so you are, my thrice renowned liege.","sourceLine":"4.2.14","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ha! am I king? 'tis so: but Edward lives.","sourceLine":"4.2.15","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True, noble prince.","sourceLine":"4.2.16","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O bitter consequence,\nThat Edward still should live! 'True, noble prince!'\nCousin, thou wert not wont to be so dull:\nShall I be plain? I wish the bastards dead;\nAnd I would have it suddenly perform'd.\nWhat sayest thou? speak suddenly; be brief.","sourceLine":"4.2.17","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your grace may do your pleasure.","sourceLine":"4.2.23","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, tut, thou art all ice, thy kindness freezeth:\nSay, have I thy consent that they shall die?","sourceLine":"4.2.24","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me some breath, some little pause, my lord\nBefore I positively herein:\nI will resolve your grace immediately.","sourceLine":"4.2.26","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to a stander by]\nThe king is angry: see, he bites the lip.","sourceLine":"4.2.29","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will converse with iron-witted fools\nAnd unrespective boys: none are for me\nThat look into me with considerate eyes:\nHigh-reaching Buckingham grows circumspect.\nBoy!","sourceLine":"4.2.31","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"4.2.36","speaker":"Page","id":"richardiii:4.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Know'st thou not any whom corrupting gold\nWould tempt unto a close exploit of death?","sourceLine":"4.2.37","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I know a discontented gentleman,\nWhose humble means match not his haughty mind:\nGold were as good as twenty orators,\nAnd will, no doubt, tempt him to any thing.","sourceLine":"4.2.39","speaker":"Page","id":"richardiii:4.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is his name?","sourceLine":"4.2.43","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His name, my lord, is Tyrrel.","sourceLine":"4.2.44","speaker":"Page","id":"richardiii:4.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I partly know the man: go, call him hither.","sourceLine":"4.2.45","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Page","id":"richardiii:4.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The deep-revolving witty Buckingham\nNo more shall be the neighbour to my counsel:\nHath he so long held out with me untired,\nAnd stops he now for breath?","sourceLine":"4.2.46","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! what news with you?","sourceLine":"4.2.50","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I hear the Marquis Dorset's fled\nTo Richmond, in those parts beyond the sea\nWhere he abides.","sourceLine":"4.2.51","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.2:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Stands apart","id":"richardiii:4.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Catesby!","sourceLine":"4.2.54","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"4.2.55","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rumour it abroad\nThat Anne, my wife, is sick and like to die:\nI will take order for her keeping close.\nInquire me out some mean-born gentleman,\nWhom I will marry straight to Clarence' daughter:\nThe boy is foolish, and I fear not him.\nLook, how thou dream'st! I say again, give out\nThat Anne my wife is sick and like to die:\nAbout it; for it stands me much upon,\nTo stop all hopes whose growth may damage me.","sourceLine":"4.2.56","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must be married to my brother's daughter,\nOr else my kingdom stands on brittle glass.\nMurder her brothers, and then marry her!\nUncertain way of gain! But I am in\nSo far in blood that sin will pluck on sin:\nTear-falling pity dwells not in this eye.","sourceLine":"4.2.66","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Page, with TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is thy name Tyrrel?","sourceLine":"4.2.72","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"James Tyrrel, and your most obedient subject.","sourceLine":"4.2.73","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art thou, indeed?","sourceLine":"4.2.74","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Prove me, my gracious sovereign.","sourceLine":"4.2.75","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Darest thou resolve to kill a friend of mine?","sourceLine":"4.2.76","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my lord;\nBut I had rather kill two enemies.","sourceLine":"4.2.77","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, there thou hast it: two deep enemies,\nFoes to my rest and my sweet sleep's disturbers\nAre they that I would have thee deal upon:\nTyrrel, I mean those bastards in the Tower.","sourceLine":"4.2.79","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me have open means to come to them,\nAnd soon I'll rid you from the fear of them.","sourceLine":"4.2.83","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou sing'st sweet music. Hark, come hither, Tyrrel\nGo, by this token: rise, and lend thine ear:","sourceLine":"4.2.85","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:47"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Whispers","id":"richardiii:4.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is no more but so: say it is done,\nAnd I will love thee, and prefer thee too.","sourceLine":"4.2.87","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis done, my gracious lord.","sourceLine":"4.2.89","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall we hear from thee, Tyrrel, ere we sleep?","sourceLine":"4.2.90","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ye shall, my Lord.","sourceLine":"4.2.91","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.2:52"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.2:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord, I have consider'd in my mind\nThe late demand that you did sound me in.","sourceLine":"4.2.92","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, let that pass. Dorset is fled to Richmond.","sourceLine":"4.2.94","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hear that news, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.2.95","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stanley, he is your wife's son well, look to it.","sourceLine":"4.2.96","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I claim your gift, my due by promise,\nFor which your honour and your faith is pawn'd;\nThe earldom of Hereford and the moveables\nThe which you promised I should possess.","sourceLine":"4.2.97","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stanley, look to your wife; if she convey\nLetters to Richmond, you shall answer it.","sourceLine":"4.2.101","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What says your highness to my just demand?","sourceLine":"4.2.103","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As I remember, Henry the Sixth\nDid prophesy that Richmond should be king,\nWhen Richmond was a little peevish boy.\nA king, perhaps, perhaps,--","sourceLine":"4.2.104","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord!","sourceLine":"4.2.108","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How chance the prophet could not at that time\nHave told me, I being by, that I should kill him?","sourceLine":"4.2.109","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, your promise for the earldom,--","sourceLine":"4.2.111","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Richmond! When last I was at Exeter,\nThe mayor in courtesy show'd me the castle,\nAnd call'd it Rougemont: at which name I started,\nBecause a bard of Ireland told me once\nI should not live long after I saw Richmond.","sourceLine":"4.2.112","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord!","sourceLine":"4.2.117","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, what's o'clock?","sourceLine":"4.2.118","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am thus bold to put your grace in mind\nOf what you promised me.","sourceLine":"4.2.119","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, but what's o'clock?","sourceLine":"4.2.121","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon the stroke of ten.","sourceLine":"4.2.122","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, let it strike.","sourceLine":"4.2.123","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why let it strike?","sourceLine":"4.2.124","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because that, like a Jack, thou keep'st the stroke\nBetwixt thy begging and my meditation.\nI am not in the giving vein to-day.","sourceLine":"4.2.125","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then resolve me whether you will or no.","sourceLine":"4.2.128","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tut, tut,\nThou troublest me; am not in the vein.","sourceLine":"4.2.129","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.2:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it even so? rewards he my true service\nWith such deep contempt made I him king for this?\nO, let me think on Hastings, and be gone\nTo Brecknock, while my fearful head is on!","sourceLine":"4.2.131","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:4.2:78"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.2:79"}],"scene":2},{"id":"4.3","label":"Act 4 · SCENE III. The same.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The tyrannous and bloody deed is done.\nThe most arch of piteous massacre\nThat ever yet this land was guilty of.\nDighton and Forrest, whom I did suborn\nTo do this ruthless piece of butchery,\nAlthough they were flesh'd villains, bloody dogs,\nMelting with tenderness and kind compassion\nWept like two children in their deaths' sad stories.\n'Lo, thus' quoth Dighton, 'lay those tender babes:'\n'Thus, thus,' quoth Forrest, 'girdling one another\nWithin their innocent alabaster arms:\nTheir lips were four red roses on a stalk,\nWhich in their summer beauty kiss'd each other.\nA book of prayers on their pillow lay;\nWhich once,' quoth Forrest, 'almost changed my mind;\nBut O! the devil'--there the villain stopp'd\nWhilst Dighton thus told on: 'We smothered\nThe most replenished sweet work of nature,\nThat from the prime creation e'er she framed.'\nThus both are gone with conscience and remorse;\nThey could not speak; and so I left them both,\nTo bring this tidings to the bloody king.\nAnd here he comes.","sourceLine":"4.3.1","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All hail, my sovereign liege!","sourceLine":"4.3.24","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Kind Tyrrel, am I happy in thy news?","sourceLine":"4.3.25","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If to have done the thing you gave in charge\nBeget your happiness, be happy then,\nFor it is done, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.3.26","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But didst thou see them dead?","sourceLine":"4.3.29","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.3.30","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And buried, gentle Tyrrel?","sourceLine":"4.3.31","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The chaplain of the Tower hath buried them;\nBut how or in what place I do not know.","sourceLine":"4.3.32","speaker":"TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come to me, Tyrrel, soon at after supper,\nAnd thou shalt tell the process of their death.\nMeantime, but think how I may do thee good,\nAnd be inheritor of thy desire.\nFarewell till soon.","sourceLine":"4.3.34","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit TYRREL","id":"richardiii:4.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The son of Clarence have I pent up close;\nHis daughter meanly have I match'd in marriage;\nThe sons of Edward sleep in Abraham's bosom,\nAnd Anne my wife hath bid the world good night.\nNow, for I know the Breton Richmond aims\nAt young Elizabeth, my brother's daughter,\nAnd, by that knot, looks proudly o'er the crown,\nTo her I go, a jolly thriving wooer.","sourceLine":"4.3.39","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord!","sourceLine":"4.3.47","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good news or bad, that thou comest in so bluntly?","sourceLine":"4.3.48","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bad news, my lord: Ely is fled to Richmond;\nAnd Buckingham, back'd with the hardy Welshmen,\nIs in the field, and still his power increaseth.","sourceLine":"4.3.49","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ely with Richmond troubles me more near\nThan Buckingham and his rash-levied army.\nCome, I have heard that fearful commenting\nIs leaden servitor to dull delay;\nDelay leads impotent and snail-paced beggary\nThen fiery expedition be my wing,\nJove's Mercury, and herald for a king!\nCome, muster men: my counsel is my shield;\nWe must be brief when traitors brave the field.","sourceLine":"4.3.52","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.3:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:4.3:19"}],"scene":3},{"id":"4.4","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IV. Before the palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, now prosperity begins to mellow\nAnd drop into the rotten mouth of death.\nHere in these confines slily have I lurk'd,\nTo watch the waning of mine adversaries.\nA dire induction am I witness to,\nAnd will to France, hoping the consequence\nWill prove as bitter, black, and tragical.\nWithdraw thee, wretched Margaret: who comes here?","sourceLine":"4.4.1","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter QUEEN ELIZABETH and the DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, my young princes! ah, my tender babes!\nMy unblown flowers, new-appearing sweets!\nIf yet your gentle souls fly in the air\nAnd be not fix'd in doom perpetual,\nHover about me with your airy wings\nAnd hear your mother's lamentation!","sourceLine":"4.4.9","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hover about her; say, that right for right\nHath dimm'd your infant morn to aged night.","sourceLine":"4.4.15","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So many miseries have crazed my voice,\nThat my woe-wearied tongue is mute and dumb,\nEdward Plantagenet, why art thou dead?","sourceLine":"4.4.17","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Plantagenet doth quit Plantagenet.\nEdward for Edward pays a dying debt.","sourceLine":"4.4.20","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou, O God, fly from such gentle lambs,\nAnd throw them in the entrails of the wolf?\nWhen didst thou sleep when such a deed was done?","sourceLine":"4.4.22","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When holy Harry died, and my sweet son.","sourceLine":"4.4.25","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Blind sight, dead life, poor mortal living ghost,\nWoe's scene, world's shame, grave's due by life usurp'd,\nBrief abstract and record of tedious days,\nRest thy unrest on England's lawful earth,","sourceLine":"4.4.26","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sitting down","id":"richardiii:4.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unlawfully made drunk with innocents' blood!","sourceLine":"4.4.30","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, that thou wouldst as well afford a grave\nAs thou canst yield a melancholy seat!\nThen would I hide my bones, not rest them here.\nO, who hath any cause to mourn but I?","sourceLine":"4.4.31","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sitting down by her","id":"richardiii:4.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If ancient sorrow be most reverend,\nGive mine the benefit of seniory,\nAnd let my woes frown on the upper hand.\nIf sorrow can admit society,","sourceLine":"4.4.35","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sitting down with them","id":"richardiii:4.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell o'er your woes again by viewing mine:\nI had an Edward, till a Richard kill'd him;\nI had a Harry, till a Richard kill'd him:\nThou hadst an Edward, till a Richard kill'd him;\nThou hadst a Richard, till a Richard killed him;","sourceLine":"4.4.39","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had a Richard too, and thou didst kill him;\nI had a Rutland too, thou holp'st to kill him.","sourceLine":"4.4.44","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hadst a Clarence too, and Richard kill'd him.\nFrom forth the kennel of thy womb hath crept\nA hell-hound that doth hunt us all to death:\nThat dog, that had his teeth before his eyes,\nTo worry lambs and lap their gentle blood,\nThat foul defacer of God's handiwork,\nThat excellent grand tyrant of the earth,\nThat reigns in galled eyes of weeping souls,\nThy womb let loose, to chase us to our graves.\nO upright, just, and true-disposing God,\nHow do I thank thee, that this carnal cur\nPreys on the issue of his mother's body,\nAnd makes her pew-fellow with others' moan!","sourceLine":"4.4.46","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Harry's wife, triumph not in my woes!\nGod witness with me, I have wept for thine.","sourceLine":"4.4.59","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear with me; I am hungry for revenge,\nAnd now I cloy me with beholding it.\nThy Edward he is dead, that stabb'd my Edward:\nThy other Edward dead, to quit my Edward;\nYoung York he is but boot, because both they\nMatch not the high perfection of my loss:\nThy Clarence he is dead that kill'd my Edward;\nAnd the beholders of this tragic play,\nThe adulterate Hastings, Rivers, Vaughan, Grey,\nUntimely smother'd in their dusky graves.\nRichard yet lives, hell's black intelligencer,\nOnly reserved their factor, to buy souls\nAnd send them thither: but at hand, at hand,\nEnsues his piteous and unpitied end:\nEarth gapes, hell burns, fiends roar, saints pray.\nTo have him suddenly convey'd away.\nCancel his bond of life, dear God, I prey,\nThat I may live to say, The dog is dead!","sourceLine":"4.4.61","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, thou didst prophesy the time would come\nThat I should wish for thee to help me curse\nThat bottled spider, that foul bunch-back'd toad!","sourceLine":"4.4.79","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I call'd thee then vain flourish of my fortune;\nI call'd thee then poor shadow, painted queen;\nThe presentation of but what I was;\nThe flattering index of a direful pageant;\nOne heaved a-high, to be hurl'd down below;\nA mother only mock'd with two sweet babes;\nA dream of what thou wert, a breath, a bubble,\nA sign of dignity, a garish flag,\nTo be the aim of every dangerous shot,\nA queen in jest, only to fill the scene.\nWhere is thy husband now? where be thy brothers?\nWhere are thy children? wherein dost thou, joy?\nWho sues to thee and cries 'God save the queen'?\nWhere be the bending peers that flatter'd thee?\nWhere be the thronging troops that follow'd thee?\nDecline all this, and see what now thou art:\nFor happy wife, a most distressed widow;\nFor joyful mother, one that wails the name;\nFor queen, a very caitiff crown'd with care;\nFor one being sued to, one that humbly sues;\nFor one that scorn'd at me, now scorn'd of me;\nFor one being fear'd of all, now fearing one;\nFor one commanding all, obey'd of none.\nThus hath the course of justice wheel'd about,\nAnd left thee but a very prey to time;\nHaving no more but thought of what thou wert,\nTo torture thee the more, being what thou art.\nThou didst usurp my place, and dost thou not\nUsurp the just proportion of my sorrow?\nNow thy proud neck bears half my burthen'd yoke;\nFrom which even here I slip my weary neck,\nAnd leave the burthen of it all on thee.\nFarewell, York's wife, and queen of sad mischance:\nThese English woes will make me smile in France.","sourceLine":"4.4.82","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O thou well skill'd in curses, stay awhile,\nAnd teach me how to curse mine enemies!","sourceLine":"4.4.116","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forbear to sleep the nights, and fast the days;\nCompare dead happiness with living woe;\nThink that thy babes were fairer than they were,\nAnd he that slew them fouler than he is:\nBettering thy loss makes the bad causer worse:\nRevolving this will teach thee how to curse.","sourceLine":"4.4.118","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My words are dull; O, quicken them with thine!","sourceLine":"4.4.124","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy woes will make them sharp, and pierce like mine.","sourceLine":"4.4.125","speaker":"QUEEN MARGARET","id":"richardiii:4.4:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.4:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should calamity be full of words?","sourceLine":"4.4.126","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Windy attorneys to their client woes,\nAiry succeeders of intestate joys,\nPoor breathing orators of miseries!\nLet them have scope: though what they do impart\nHelp not all, yet do they ease the heart.","sourceLine":"4.4.127","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If so, then be not tongue-tied: go with me.\nAnd in the breath of bitter words let's smother\nMy damned son, which thy two sweet sons smother'd.\nI hear his drum: be copious in exclaims.","sourceLine":"4.4.132","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING RICHARD III, marching, with drums and trumpets","id":"richardiii:4.4:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who intercepts my expedition?","sourceLine":"4.4.136","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, she that might have intercepted thee,\nBy strangling thee in her accursed womb\nFrom all the slaughters, wretch, that thou hast done!","sourceLine":"4.4.137","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hidest thou that forehead with a golden crown,\nWhere should be graven, if that right were right,\nThe slaughter of the prince that owed that crown,\nAnd the dire death of my two sons and brothers?\nTell me, thou villain slave, where are my children?","sourceLine":"4.4.140","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou toad, thou toad, where is thy brother Clarence?\nAnd little Ned Plantagenet, his son?","sourceLine":"4.4.145","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is kind Hastings, Rivers, Vaughan, Grey?","sourceLine":"4.4.147","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A flourish, trumpets! strike alarum, drums!\nLet not the heavens hear these tell-tale women\nRail on the Lord's enointed: strike, I say!","sourceLine":"4.4.148","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Alarums","id":"richardiii:4.4:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Either be patient, and entreat me fair,\nOr with the clamorous report of war\nThus will I drown your exclamations.","sourceLine":"4.4.151","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art thou my son?","sourceLine":"4.4.154","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, I thank God, my father, and yourself.","sourceLine":"4.4.155","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then patiently hear my impatience.","sourceLine":"4.4.156","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I have a touch of your condition,\nWhich cannot brook the accent of reproof.","sourceLine":"4.4.157","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, let me speak!","sourceLine":"4.4.159","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do then: but I'll not hear.","sourceLine":"4.4.160","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will be mild and gentle in my speech.","sourceLine":"4.4.161","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And brief, good mother; for I am in haste.","sourceLine":"4.4.162","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art thou so hasty? I have stay'd for thee,\nGod knows, in anguish, pain and agony.","sourceLine":"4.4.163","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And came I not at last to comfort you?","sourceLine":"4.4.165","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, by the holy rood, thou know'st it well,\nThou camest on earth to make the earth my hell.\nA grievous burthen was thy birth to me;\nTetchy and wayward was thy infancy;\nThy school-days frightful, desperate, wild, and furious,\nThy prime of manhood daring, bold, and venturous,\nThy age confirm'd, proud, subdued, bloody,\ntreacherous,\nMore mild, but yet more harmful, kind in hatred:\nWhat comfortable hour canst thou name,\nThat ever graced me in thy company?","sourceLine":"4.4.166","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, none, but Humphrey Hour, that call'd\nyour grace\nTo breakfast once forth of my company.\nIf I be so disgracious in your sight,\nLet me march on, and not offend your grace.\nStrike the drum.","sourceLine":"4.4.177","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I prithee, hear me speak.","sourceLine":"4.4.183","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You speak too bitterly.","sourceLine":"4.4.184","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear me a word;\nFor I shall never speak to thee again.","sourceLine":"4.4.185","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So.","sourceLine":"4.4.187","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Either thou wilt die, by God's just ordinance,\nEre from this war thou turn a conqueror,\nOr I with grief and extreme age shall perish\nAnd never look upon thy face again.\nTherefore take with thee my most heavy curse;\nWhich, in the day of battle, tire thee more\nThan all the complete armour that thou wear'st!\nMy prayers on the adverse party fight;\nAnd there the little souls of Edward's children\nWhisper the spirits of thine enemies\nAnd promise them success and victory.\nBloody thou art, bloody will be thy end;\nShame serves thy life and doth thy death attend.","sourceLine":"4.4.188","speaker":"DUCHESS OF YORK","id":"richardiii:4.4:57"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.4:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though far more cause, yet much less spirit to curse\nAbides in me; I say amen to all.","sourceLine":"4.4.201","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay, madam; I must speak a word with you.","sourceLine":"4.4.203","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have no more sons of the royal blood\nFor thee to murder: for my daughters, Richard,\nThey shall be praying nuns, not weeping queens;\nAnd therefore level not to hit their lives.","sourceLine":"4.4.204","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have a daughter call'd Elizabeth,\nVirtuous and fair, royal and gracious.","sourceLine":"4.4.208","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And must she die for this? O, let her live,\nAnd I'll corrupt her manners, stain her beauty;\nSlander myself as false to Edward's bed;\nThrow over her the veil of infamy:\nSo she may live unscarr'd of bleeding slaughter,\nI will confess she was not Edward's daughter.","sourceLine":"4.4.210","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wrong not her birth, she is of royal blood.","sourceLine":"4.4.216","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To save her life, I'll say she is not so.","sourceLine":"4.4.217","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her life is only safest in her birth.","sourceLine":"4.4.218","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And only in that safety died her brothers.","sourceLine":"4.4.219","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lo, at their births good stars were opposite.","sourceLine":"4.4.220","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, to their lives bad friends were contrary.","sourceLine":"4.4.221","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All unavoided is the doom of destiny.","sourceLine":"4.4.222","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True, when avoided grace makes destiny:\nMy babes were destined to a fairer death,\nIf grace had bless'd thee with a fairer life.","sourceLine":"4.4.223","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You speak as if that I had slain my cousins.","sourceLine":"4.4.226","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cousins, indeed; and by their uncle cozen'd\nOf comfort, kingdom, kindred, freedom, life.\nWhose hand soever lanced their tender hearts,\nThy head, all indirectly, gave direction:\nNo doubt the murderous knife was dull and blunt\nTill it was whetted on thy stone-hard heart,\nTo revel in the entrails of my lambs.\nBut that still use of grief makes wild grief tame,\nMy tongue should to thy ears not name my boys\nTill that my nails were anchor'd in thine eyes;\nAnd I, in such a desperate bay of death,\nLike a poor bark, of sails and tackling reft,\nRush all to pieces on thy rocky bosom.","sourceLine":"4.4.227","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, so thrive I in my enterprise\nAnd dangerous success of bloody wars,\nAs I intend more good to you and yours,\nThan ever you or yours were by me wrong'd!","sourceLine":"4.4.240","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What good is cover'd with the face of heaven,\nTo be discover'd, that can do me good?","sourceLine":"4.4.244","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The advancement of your children, gentle lady.","sourceLine":"4.4.246","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Up to some scaffold, there to lose their heads?","sourceLine":"4.4.247","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, to the dignity and height of honour\nThe high imperial type of this earth's glory.","sourceLine":"4.4.248","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Flatter my sorrows with report of it;\nTell me what state, what dignity, what honour,\nCanst thou demise to any child of mine?","sourceLine":"4.4.250","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even all I have; yea, and myself and all,\nWill I withal endow a child of thine;\nSo in the Lethe of thy angry soul\nThou drown the sad remembrance of those wrongs\nWhich thou supposest I have done to thee.","sourceLine":"4.4.253","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be brief, lest that be process of thy kindness\nLast longer telling than thy kindness' date.","sourceLine":"4.4.258","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then know, that from my soul I love thy daughter.","sourceLine":"4.4.260","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My daughter's mother thinks it with her soul.","sourceLine":"4.4.261","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What do you think?","sourceLine":"4.4.262","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That thou dost love my daughter from thy soul:\nSo from thy soul's love didst thou love her brothers;\nAnd from my heart's love I do thank thee for it.","sourceLine":"4.4.263","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be not so hasty to confound my meaning:\nI mean, that with my soul I love thy daughter,\nAnd mean to make her queen of England.","sourceLine":"4.4.266","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say then, who dost thou mean shall be her king?","sourceLine":"4.4.269","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even he that makes her queen who should be else?","sourceLine":"4.4.270","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, thou?","sourceLine":"4.4.271","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I, even I: what think you of it, madam?","sourceLine":"4.4.272","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How canst thou woo her?","sourceLine":"4.4.273","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That would I learn of you,\nAs one that are best acquainted with her humour.","sourceLine":"4.4.274","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And wilt thou learn of me?","sourceLine":"4.4.276","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, with all my heart.","sourceLine":"4.4.277","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Send to her, by the man that slew her brothers,\nA pair of bleeding-hearts; thereon engrave\nEdward and York; then haply she will weep:\nTherefore present to her--as sometime Margaret\nDid to thy father, steep'd in Rutland's blood,--\nA handkerchief; which, say to her, did drain\nThe purple sap from her sweet brother's body\nAnd bid her dry her weeping eyes therewith.\nIf this inducement force her not to love,\nSend her a story of thy noble acts;\nTell her thou madest away her uncle Clarence,\nHer uncle Rivers; yea, and, for her sake,\nMadest quick conveyance with her good aunt Anne.","sourceLine":"4.4.278","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, you mock me; this is not the way\nTo win our daughter.","sourceLine":"4.4.291","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is no other way\nUnless thou couldst put on some other shape,\nAnd not be Richard that hath done all this.","sourceLine":"4.4.293","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say that I did all this for love of her.","sourceLine":"4.4.296","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then indeed she cannot choose but hate thee,\nHaving bought love with such a bloody spoil.","sourceLine":"4.4.297","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look, what is done cannot be now amended:\nMen shall deal unadvisedly sometimes,\nWhich after hours give leisure to repent.\nIf I did take the kingdom from your sons,\nTo make amends, Ill give it to your daughter.\nIf I have kill'd the issue of your womb,\nTo quicken your increase, I will beget\nMine issue of your blood upon your daughter\nA grandam's name is little less in love\nThan is the doting title of a mother;\nThey are as children but one step below,\nEven of your mettle, of your very blood;\nOf an one pain, save for a night of groans\nEndured of her, for whom you bid like sorrow.\nYour children were vexation to your youth,\nBut mine shall be a comfort to your age.\nThe loss you have is but a son being king,\nAnd by that loss your daughter is made queen.\nI cannot make you what amends I would,\nTherefore accept such kindness as I can.\nDorset your son, that with a fearful soul\nLeads discontented steps in foreign soil,\nThis fair alliance quickly shall call home\nTo high promotions and great dignity:\nThe king, that calls your beauteous daughter wife.\nFamiliarly shall call thy Dorset brother;\nAgain shall you be mother to a king,\nAnd all the ruins of distressful times\nRepair'd with double riches of content.\nWhat! we have many goodly days to see:\nThe liquid drops of tears that you have shed\nShall come again, transform'd to orient pearl,\nAdvantaging their loan with interest\nOf ten times double gain of happiness.\nGo, then my mother, to thy daughter go\nMake bold her bashful years with your experience;\nPrepare her ears to hear a wooer's tale\nPut in her tender heart the aspiring flame\nOf golden sovereignty; acquaint the princess\nWith the sweet silent hours of marriage joys\nAnd when this arm of mine hath chastised\nThe petty rebel, dull-brain'd Buckingham,\nBound with triumphant garlands will I come\nAnd lead thy daughter to a conqueror's bed;\nTo whom I will retail my conquest won,\nAnd she shall be sole victress, Caesar's Caesar.","sourceLine":"4.4.299","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What were I best to say? her father's brother\nWould be her lord? or shall I say, her uncle?\nOr, he that slew her brothers and her uncles?\nUnder what title shall I woo for thee,\nThat God, the law, my honour and her love,\nCan make seem pleasing to her tender years?","sourceLine":"4.4.345","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Infer fair England's peace by this alliance.","sourceLine":"4.4.351","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which she shall purchase with still lasting war.","sourceLine":"4.4.352","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say that the king, which may command, entreats.","sourceLine":"4.4.353","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That at her hands which the king's King forbids.","sourceLine":"4.4.354","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, she shall be a high and mighty queen.","sourceLine":"4.4.355","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To wail the tide, as her mother doth.","sourceLine":"4.4.356","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, I will love her everlastingly.","sourceLine":"4.4.357","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But how long shall that title 'ever' last?","sourceLine":"4.4.358","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweetly in force unto her fair life's end.","sourceLine":"4.4.359","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But how long fairly shall her sweet lie last?","sourceLine":"4.4.360","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So long as heaven and nature lengthens it.","sourceLine":"4.4.361","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So long as hell and Richard likes of it.","sourceLine":"4.4.362","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, I, her sovereign, am her subject love.","sourceLine":"4.4.363","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But she, your subject, loathes such sovereignty.","sourceLine":"4.4.364","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be eloquent in my behalf to her.","sourceLine":"4.4.365","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An honest tale speeds best being plainly told.","sourceLine":"4.4.366","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then in plain terms tell her my loving tale.","sourceLine":"4.4.367","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Plain and not honest is too harsh a style.","sourceLine":"4.4.368","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your reasons are too shallow and too quick.","sourceLine":"4.4.369","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O no, my reasons are too deep and dead;\nToo deep and dead, poor infants, in their grave.","sourceLine":"4.4.370","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Harp not on that string, madam; that is past.","sourceLine":"4.4.372","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Harp on it still shall I till heart-strings break.","sourceLine":"4.4.373","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by my George, my garter, and my crown,--","sourceLine":"4.4.374","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:124"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Profaned, dishonour'd, and the third usurp'd.","sourceLine":"4.4.375","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I swear--","sourceLine":"4.4.376","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:126"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By nothing; for this is no oath:\nThe George, profaned, hath lost his holy honour;\nThe garter, blemish'd, pawn'd his knightly virtue;\nThe crown, usurp'd, disgraced his kingly glory.\nif something thou wilt swear to be believed,\nSwear then by something that thou hast not wrong'd.","sourceLine":"4.4.377","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:127"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by the world--","sourceLine":"4.4.383","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:128"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis full of thy foul wrongs.","sourceLine":"4.4.384","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:129"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My father's death--","sourceLine":"4.4.385","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:130"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy life hath that dishonour'd.","sourceLine":"4.4.386","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:131"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, by myself--","sourceLine":"4.4.387","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:132"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thyself thyself misusest.","sourceLine":"4.4.388","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:133"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why then, by God--","sourceLine":"4.4.389","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:134"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God's wrong is most of all.\nIf thou hadst fear'd to break an oath by Him,\nThe unity the king thy brother made\nHad not been broken, nor my brother slain:\nIf thou hadst fear'd to break an oath by Him,\nThe imperial metal, circling now thy brow,\nHad graced the tender temples of my child,\nAnd both the princes had been breathing here,\nWhich now, two tender playfellows to dust,\nThy broken faith hath made a prey for worms.\nWhat canst thou swear by now?","sourceLine":"4.4.390","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:135"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The time to come.","sourceLine":"4.4.401","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:136"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That thou hast wronged in the time o'erpast;\nFor I myself have many tears to wash\nHereafter time, for time past wrong'd by thee.\nThe children live, whose parents thou hast\nslaughter'd,\nUngovern'd youth, to wail it in their age;\nThe parents live, whose children thou hast butcher'd,\nOld wither'd plants, to wail it with their age.\nSwear not by time to come; for that thou hast\nMisused ere used, by time misused o'erpast.","sourceLine":"4.4.402","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:137"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As I intend to prosper and repent,\nSo thrive I in my dangerous attempt\nOf hostile arms! myself myself confound!\nHeaven and fortune bar me happy hours!\nDay, yield me not thy light; nor, night, thy rest!\nBe opposite all planets of good luck\nTo my proceedings, if, with pure heart's love,\nImmaculate devotion, holy thoughts,\nI tender not thy beauteous princely daughter!\nIn her consists my happiness and thine;\nWithout her, follows to this land and me,\nTo thee, herself, and many a Christian soul,\nDeath, desolation, ruin and decay:\nIt cannot be avoided but by this;\nIt will not be avoided but by this.\nTherefore, good mother,--I must can you so--\nBe the attorney of my love to her:\nPlead what I will be, not what I have been;\nNot my deserts, but what I will deserve:\nUrge the necessity and state of times,\nAnd be not peevish-fond in great designs.","sourceLine":"4.4.412","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:138"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall I be tempted of the devil thus?","sourceLine":"4.4.433","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:139"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, if the devil tempt thee to do good.","sourceLine":"4.4.434","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:140"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall I forget myself to be myself?","sourceLine":"4.4.435","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:141"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, if yourself's remembrance wrong yourself.","sourceLine":"4.4.436","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:142"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But thou didst kill my children.","sourceLine":"4.4.437","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:143"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But in your daughter's womb I bury them:\nWhere in that nest of spicery they shall breed\nSelves of themselves, to your recomforture.","sourceLine":"4.4.438","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:144"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall I go win my daughter to thy will?","sourceLine":"4.4.441","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:145"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And be a happy mother by the deed.","sourceLine":"4.4.442","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:146"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go. Write to me very shortly.\nAnd you shall understand from me her mind.","sourceLine":"4.4.443","speaker":"QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:147"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear her my true love's kiss; and so, farewell.","sourceLine":"4.4.445","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:148"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit QUEEN ELIZABETH","id":"richardiii:4.4:149"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Relenting fool, and shallow, changing woman!","sourceLine":"4.4.446","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:150"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter RATCLIFF; CATESBY following","id":"richardiii:4.4:151"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! what news?","sourceLine":"4.4.447","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:152"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious sovereign, on the western coast\nRideth a puissant navy; to the shore\nThrong many doubtful hollow-hearted friends,\nUnarm'd, and unresolved to beat them back:\n'Tis thought that Richmond is their admiral;\nAnd there they hull, expecting but the aid\nOf Buckingham to welcome them ashore.","sourceLine":"4.4.448","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:4.4:153"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some light-foot friend post to the Duke of Norfolk:\nRatcliff, thyself, or Catesby; where is he?","sourceLine":"4.4.455","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:154"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.4.457","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:155"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fly to the duke:","sourceLine":"4.4.458","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:156"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:4.4:157"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Post thou to Salisbury\nWhen thou comest thither--","sourceLine":"4.4.459","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:158"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:159"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dull, unmindful villain,\nWhy stand'st thou still, and go'st not to the duke?","sourceLine":"4.4.461","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:160"},{"kind":"speech","text":"First, mighty sovereign, let me know your mind,\nWhat from your grace I shall deliver to him.","sourceLine":"4.4.463","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:161"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, true, good Catesby: bid him levy straight\nThe greatest strength and power he can make,\nAnd meet me presently at Salisbury.","sourceLine":"4.4.465","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:162"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go.","sourceLine":"4.4.468","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:163"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.4:164"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is't your highness' pleasure I shall do at\nSalisbury?","sourceLine":"4.4.469","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:4.4:165"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, what wouldst thou do there before I go?","sourceLine":"4.4.471","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:166"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your highness told me I should post before.","sourceLine":"4.4.472","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:4.4:167"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My mind is changed, sir, my mind is changed.","sourceLine":"4.4.473","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:168"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:169"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, what news with you?","sourceLine":"4.4.474","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:170"},{"kind":"speech","text":"None good, my lord, to please you with the hearing;\nNor none so bad, but it may well be told.","sourceLine":"4.4.475","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:171"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hoyday, a riddle! neither good nor bad!\nWhy dost thou run so many mile about,\nWhen thou mayst tell thy tale a nearer way?\nOnce more, what news?","sourceLine":"4.4.477","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:172"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Richmond is on the seas.","sourceLine":"4.4.481","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:173"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There let him sink, and be the seas on him!\nWhite-liver'd runagate, what doth he there?","sourceLine":"4.4.482","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:174"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not, mighty sovereign, but by guess.","sourceLine":"4.4.484","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:175"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir, as you guess, as you guess?","sourceLine":"4.4.485","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:176"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stirr'd up by Dorset, Buckingham, and Ely,\nHe makes for England, there to claim the crown.","sourceLine":"4.4.486","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:177"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is the chair empty? is the sword unsway'd?\nIs the king dead? the empire unpossess'd?\nWhat heir of York is there alive but we?\nAnd who is England's king but great York's heir?\nThen, tell me, what doth he upon the sea?","sourceLine":"4.4.488","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:178"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unless for that, my liege, I cannot guess.","sourceLine":"4.4.493","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:179"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unless for that he comes to be your liege,\nYou cannot guess wherefore the Welshman comes.\nThou wilt revolt, and fly to him, I fear.","sourceLine":"4.4.494","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:180"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, mighty liege; therefore mistrust me not.","sourceLine":"4.4.497","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:181"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is thy power, then, to beat him back?\nWhere are thy tenants and thy followers?\nAre they not now upon the western shore.\nSafe-conducting the rebels from their ships!","sourceLine":"4.4.498","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:182"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my good lord, my friends are in the north.","sourceLine":"4.4.502","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:183"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cold friends to Richard: what do they in the north,\nWhen they should serve their sovereign in the west?","sourceLine":"4.4.503","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:184"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They have not been commanded, mighty sovereign:\nPlease it your majesty to give me leave,\nI'll muster up my friends, and meet your grace\nWhere and what time your majesty shall please.","sourceLine":"4.4.505","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:185"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, ay. thou wouldst be gone to join with Richmond:\nI will not trust you, sir.","sourceLine":"4.4.509","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:186"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most mighty sovereign,\nYou have no cause to hold my friendship doubtful:\nI never was nor never will be false.","sourceLine":"4.4.511","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:187"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well,\nGo muster men; but, hear you, leave behind\nYour son, George Stanley: look your faith be firm.\nOr else his head's assurance is but frail.","sourceLine":"4.4.514","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:188"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So deal with him as I prove true to you.","sourceLine":"4.4.518","speaker":"STANLEY","id":"richardiii:4.4:189"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:4.4:190"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:191"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious sovereign, now in Devonshire,\nAs I by friends am well advertised,\nSir Edward Courtney, and the haughty prelate\nBishop of Exeter, his brother there,\nWith many more confederates, are in arms.","sourceLine":"4.4.519","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:192"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:193"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My liege, in Kent the Guildfords are in arms;\nAnd every hour more competitors\nFlock to their aid, and still their power increaseth.","sourceLine":"4.4.524","speaker":"Second Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:194"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:195"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, the army of the Duke of Buckingham--","sourceLine":"4.4.527","speaker":"Third Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:196"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Out on you, owls! nothing but songs of death?","sourceLine":"4.4.528","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:197"},{"kind":"stage","text":"He striketh him","id":"richardiii:4.4:198"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take that, until thou bring me better news.","sourceLine":"4.4.529","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:199"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The news I have to tell your majesty\nIs, that by sudden floods and fall of waters,\nBuckingham's army is dispersed and scatter'd;\nAnd he himself wander'd away alone,\nNo man knows whither.","sourceLine":"4.4.530","speaker":"Third Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:200"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cry thee mercy:\nThere is my purse to cure that blow of thine.\nHath any well-advised friend proclaim'd\nReward to him that brings the traitor in?","sourceLine":"4.4.535","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:201"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such proclamation hath been made, my liege.","sourceLine":"4.4.539","speaker":"Third Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:202"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:203"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir Thomas Lovel and Lord Marquis Dorset,\n'Tis said, my liege, in Yorkshire are in arms.\nYet this good comfort bring I to your grace,\nThe Breton navy is dispersed by tempest:\nRichmond, in Yorkshire, sent out a boat\nUnto the shore, to ask those on the banks\nIf they were his assistants, yea or no;\nWho answer'd him, they came from Buckingham.\nUpon his party: he, mistrusting them,\nHoisted sail and made away for Brittany.","sourceLine":"4.4.540","speaker":"Fourth Messenger","id":"richardiii:4.4:204"},{"kind":"speech","text":"March on, march on, since we are up in arms;\nIf not to fight with foreign enemies,\nYet to beat down these rebels here at home.","sourceLine":"4.4.550","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:205"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:206"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My liege, the Duke of Buckingham is taken;\nThat is the best news: that the Earl of Richmond\nIs with a mighty power landed at Milford,\nIs colder tidings, yet they must be told.","sourceLine":"4.4.553","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:4.4:207"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away towards Salisbury! while we reason here,\nA royal battle might be won and lost\nSome one take order Buckingham be brought\nTo Salisbury; the rest march on with me.","sourceLine":"4.4.557","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:4.4:208"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"richardiii:4.4:209"}],"scene":4},{"id":"4.5","label":"Act 4 · SCENE V. Lord Derby's house.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DERBY and SIR CHRISTOPHER URSWICK","id":"richardiii:4.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir Christopher, tell Richmond this from me:\nThat in the sty of this most bloody boar\nMy son George Stanley is frank'd up in hold:\nIf I revolt, off goes young George's head;\nThe fear of that withholds my present aid.\nBut, tell me, where is princely Richmond now?","sourceLine":"4.5.1","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:4.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At Pembroke, or at Harford-west, in Wales.","sourceLine":"4.5.7","speaker":"CHRISTOPHER","id":"richardiii:4.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What men of name resort to him?","sourceLine":"4.5.8","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:4.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir Walter Herbert, a renowned soldier;\nSir Gilbert Talbot, Sir William Stanley;\nOxford, redoubted Pembroke, Sir James Blunt,\nAnd Rice ap Thomas with a valiant crew;\nAnd many more of noble fame and worth:\nAnd towards London they do bend their course,\nIf by the way they be not fought withal.","sourceLine":"4.5.9","speaker":"CHRISTOPHER","id":"richardiii:4.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Return unto thy lord; commend me to him:\nTell him the queen hath heartily consented\nHe shall espouse Elizabeth her daughter.\nThese letters will resolve him of my mind. Farewell.","sourceLine":"4.5.16","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:4.5:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:4.5:7"}],"scene":5},{"id":"5.1","label":"Act 5 · SCENE I. Salisbury. An open place.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Sheriff, and BUCKINGHAM, with halberds, led to execution","id":"richardiii:5.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will not King Richard let me speak with him?","sourceLine":"5.1.1","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my good lord; therefore be patient.","sourceLine":"5.1.2","speaker":"Sheriff","id":"richardiii:5.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hastings, and Edward's children, Rivers, Grey,\nHoly King Henry, and thy fair son Edward,\nVaughan, and all that have miscarried\nBy underhand corrupted foul injustice,\nIf that your moody discontented souls\nDo through the clouds behold this present hour,\nEven for revenge mock my destruction!\nThis is All-Souls' day, fellows, is it not?","sourceLine":"5.1.3","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.1.11","speaker":"Sheriff","id":"richardiii:5.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then All-Souls' day is my body's doomsday.\nThis is the day that, in King Edward's time,\nI wish't might fall on me, when I was found\nFalse to his children or his wife's allies\nThis is the day wherein I wish'd to fall\nBy the false faith of him I trusted most;\nThis, this All-Souls' day to my fearful soul\nIs the determined respite of my wrongs:\nThat high All-Seer that I dallied with\nHath turn'd my feigned prayer on my head\nAnd given in earnest what I begg'd in jest.\nThus doth he force the swords of wicked men\nTo turn their own points on their masters' bosoms:\nNow Margaret's curse is fallen upon my head;\n'When he,' quoth she, 'shall split thy heart with sorrow,\nRemember Margaret was a prophetess.'\nCome, sirs, convey me to the block of shame;\nWrong hath but wrong, and blame the due of blame.","sourceLine":"5.1.12","speaker":"BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.1:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.1:7"}],"scene":1},{"id":"5.2","label":"Act 5 · SCENE II. The camp near Tamworth.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter RICHMOND, OXFORD, BLUNT, HERBERT, and others, with drum and colours","id":"richardiii:5.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fellows in arms, and my most loving friends,\nBruised underneath the yoke of tyranny,\nThus far into the bowels of the land\nHave we march'd on without impediment;\nAnd here receive we from our father Stanley\nLines of fair comfort and encouragement.\nThe wretched, bloody, and usurping boar,\nThat spoil'd your summer fields and fruitful vines,\nSwills your warm blood like wash, and makes his trough\nIn your embowell'd bosoms, this foul swine\nLies now even in the centre of this isle,\nNear to the town of Leicester, as we learn\nFrom Tamworth thither is but one day's march.\nIn God's name, cheerly on, courageous friends,\nTo reap the harvest of perpetual peace\nBy this one bloody trial of sharp war.","sourceLine":"5.2.1","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Every man's conscience is a thousand swords,\nTo fight against that bloody homicide.","sourceLine":"5.2.17","speaker":"OXFORD","id":"richardiii:5.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I doubt not but his friends will fly to us.","sourceLine":"5.2.19","speaker":"HERBERT","id":"richardiii:5.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath no friends but who are friends for fear.\nWhich in his greatest need will shrink from him.","sourceLine":"5.2.20","speaker":"BLUNT","id":"richardiii:5.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All for our vantage. Then, in God's name, march:\nTrue hope is swift, and flies with swallow's wings:\nKings it makes gods, and meaner creatures kings.","sourceLine":"5.2.22","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.2:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.2:7"}],"scene":2},{"id":"5.3","label":"Act 5 · SCENE III. Bosworth Field.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING RICHARD III in arms, with NORFOLK, SURREY, and others","id":"richardiii:5.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here pitch our tents, even here in Bosworth field.\nMy Lord of Surrey, why look you so sad?","sourceLine":"5.3.1","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My heart is ten times lighter than my looks.","sourceLine":"5.3.3","speaker":"SURREY","id":"richardiii:5.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My Lord of Norfolk,--","sourceLine":"5.3.4","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, most gracious liege.","sourceLine":"5.3.5","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Norfolk, we must have knocks; ha! must we not?","sourceLine":"5.3.6","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We must both give and take, my gracious lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.7","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Up with my tent there! here will I lie tonight;\nBut where to-morrow? Well, all's one for that.\nWho hath descried the number of the foe?","sourceLine":"5.3.8","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Six or seven thousand is their utmost power.","sourceLine":"5.3.11","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, our battalion trebles that account:\nBesides, the king's name is a tower of strength,\nWhich they upon the adverse party want.\nUp with my tent there! Valiant gentlemen,\nLet us survey the vantage of the field\nCall for some men of sound direction\nLet's want no discipline, make no delay,\nFor, lords, to-morrow is a busy day.","sourceLine":"5.3.12","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.3:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, on the other side of the field, RICHMOND, Sir William Brandon, OXFORD, and others. Some of the Soldiers pitch RICHMOND's tent","id":"richardiii:5.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The weary sun hath made a golden set,\nAnd by the bright track of his fiery car,\nGives signal, of a goodly day to-morrow.\nSir William Brandon, you shall bear my standard.\nGive me some ink and paper in my tent\nI'll draw the form and model of our battle,\nLimit each leader to his several charge,\nAnd part in just proportion our small strength.\nMy Lord of Oxford, you, Sir William Brandon,\nAnd you, Sir Walter Herbert, stay with me.\nThe Earl of Pembroke keeps his regiment:\nGood Captain Blunt, bear my good night to him\nAnd by the second hour in the morning\nDesire the earl to see me in my tent:\nYet one thing more, good Blunt, before thou go'st,\nWhere is Lord Stanley quarter'd, dost thou know?","sourceLine":"5.3.20","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unless I have mista'en his colours much,\nWhich well I am assured I have not done,\nHis regiment lies half a mile at least\nSouth from the mighty power of the king.","sourceLine":"5.3.36","speaker":"BLUNT","id":"richardiii:5.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If without peril it be possible,\nGood Captain Blunt, bear my good-night to him,\nAnd give him from me this most needful scroll.","sourceLine":"5.3.40","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon my life, my lord, I'll under-take it;\nAnd so, God give you quiet rest to-night!","sourceLine":"5.3.43","speaker":"BLUNT","id":"richardiii:5.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good night, good Captain Blunt. Come gentlemen,\nLet us consult upon to-morrow's business\nIn to our tent; the air is raw and cold.","sourceLine":"5.3.45","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They withdraw into the tent","id":"richardiii:5.3:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, to his tent, KING RICHARD III, NORFOLK, RATCLIFF, CATESBY, and others","id":"richardiii:5.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is't o'clock?","sourceLine":"5.3.48","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It's supper-time, my lord;\nIt's nine o'clock.","sourceLine":"5.3.49","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will not sup to-night.\nGive me some ink and paper.\nWhat, is my beaver easier than it was?\nAnd all my armour laid into my tent?","sourceLine":"5.3.51","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If is, my liege; and all things are in readiness.","sourceLine":"5.3.55","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Norfolk, hie thee to thy charge;\nUse careful watch, choose trusty sentinels.","sourceLine":"5.3.56","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I go, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.58","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stir with the lark to-morrow, gentle Norfolk.","sourceLine":"5.3.59","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I warrant you, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.60","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"richardiii:5.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Catesby!","sourceLine":"5.3.61","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"5.3.62","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Send out a pursuivant at arms\nTo Stanley's regiment; bid him bring his power\nBefore sunrising, lest his son George fall\nInto the blind cave of eternal night.","sourceLine":"5.3.63","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fill me a bowl of wine. Give me a watch.\nSaddle white Surrey for the field to-morrow.\nLook that my staves be sound, and not too heavy.\nRatcliff!","sourceLine":"5.3.67","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"5.3.71","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Saw'st thou the melancholy Lord Northumberland?","sourceLine":"5.3.72","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thomas the Earl of Surrey, and himself,\nMuch about cock-shut time, from troop to troop\nWent through the army, cheering up the soldiers.","sourceLine":"5.3.73","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, I am satisfied. Give me a bowl of wine:\nI have not that alacrity of spirit,\nNor cheer of mind, that I was wont to have.\nSet it down. Is ink and paper ready?","sourceLine":"5.3.76","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.80","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bid my guard watch; leave me.\nRatcliff, about the mid of night come to my tent\nAnd help to arm me. Leave me, I say.","sourceLine":"5.3.81","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt RATCLIFF and the other Attendants","id":"richardiii:5.3:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DERBY to RICHMOND in his tent, Lords and others attending","id":"richardiii:5.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fortune and victory sit on thy helm!","sourceLine":"5.3.84","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All comfort that the dark night can afford\nBe to thy person, noble father-in-law!\nTell me, how fares our loving mother?","sourceLine":"5.3.85","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I, by attorney, bless thee from thy mother\nWho prays continually for Richmond's good:\nSo much for that. The silent hours steal on,\nAnd flaky darkness breaks within the east.\nIn brief,--for so the season bids us be,--\nPrepare thy battle early in the morning,\nAnd put thy fortune to the arbitrement\nOf bloody strokes and mortal-staring war.\nI, as I may--that which I would I cannot,--\nWith best advantage will deceive the time,\nAnd aid thee in this doubtful shock of arms:\nBut on thy side I may not be too forward\nLest, being seen, thy brother, tender George,\nBe executed in his father's sight.\nFarewell: the leisure and the fearful time\nCuts off the ceremonious vows of love\nAnd ample interchange of sweet discourse,\nWhich so long sunder'd friends should dwell upon:\nGod give us leisure for these rites of love!\nOnce more, adieu: be valiant, and speed well!","sourceLine":"5.3.88","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:5.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good lords, conduct him to his regiment:\nI'll strive, with troubled thoughts, to take a nap,\nLest leaden slumber peise me down to-morrow,\nWhen I should mount with wings of victory:\nOnce more, good night, kind lords and gentlemen.","sourceLine":"5.3.108","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:45"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Thou, whose captain I account myself,\nLook on my forces with a gracious eye;\nPut in their hands thy bruising irons of wrath,\nThat they may crush down with a heavy fall\nThe usurping helmets of our adversaries!\nMake us thy ministers of chastisement,\nThat we may praise thee in the victory!\nTo thee I do commend my watchful soul,\nEre I let fall the windows of mine eyes:\nSleeping and waking, O, defend me still!","sourceLine":"5.3.113","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:47"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sleeps","id":"richardiii:5.3:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of Prince Edward, son to King Henry VI","id":"richardiii:5.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ghost","sourceLine":"5.3.123","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nLet me sit heavy on thy soul to-morrow!\nThink, how thou stab'dst me in my prime of youth\nAt Tewksbury: despair, therefore, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.124","speaker":"of Prince Edward","id":"richardiii:5.3:51"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be cheerful, Richmond; for the wronged souls\nOf butcher'd princes fight in thy behalf\nKing Henry's issue, Richmond, comforts thee.","sourceLine":"5.3.128","speaker":"of Prince Edward","id":"richardiii:5.3:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of King Henry VI","id":"richardiii:5.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ghost","sourceLine":"5.3.131","speaker":"of Prince Edward","id":"richardiii:5.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nWhen I was mortal, my anointed body\nBy thee was punched full of deadly holes\nThink on the Tower and me: despair, and die!\nHarry the Sixth bids thee despair, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.132","speaker":"of King Henry VI","id":"richardiii:5.3:56"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Virtuous and holy, be thou conqueror!\nHarry, that prophesied thou shouldst be king,\nDoth comfort thee in thy sleep: live, and flourish!","sourceLine":"5.3.137","speaker":"of King Henry VI","id":"richardiii:5.3:58"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:5.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nLet me sit heavy on thy soul to-morrow!\nI, that was wash'd to death with fulsome wine,\nPoor Clarence, by thy guile betrayed to death!\nTo-morrow in the battle think on me,\nAnd fall thy edgeless sword: despair, and die!--","sourceLine":"5.3.140","speaker":"Ghost of CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:5.3:60"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou offspring of the house of Lancaster\nThe wronged heirs of York do pray for thee\nGood angels guard thy battle! live, and flourish!","sourceLine":"5.3.146","speaker":"Ghost of CLARENCE","id":"richardiii:5.3:62"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghosts of RIVERS, GRAY, and VAUGHAN","id":"richardiii:5.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nLet me sit heavy on thy soul to-morrow,\nRivers. that died at Pomfret! despair, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.149","speaker":"Ghost of RIVERS","id":"richardiii:5.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nThink upon Grey, and let thy soul despair!","sourceLine":"5.3.152","speaker":"Ghost of GREY","id":"richardiii:5.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nThink upon Vaughan, and, with guilty fear,\nLet fall thy lance: despair, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.154","speaker":"Ghost of VAUGHAN","id":"richardiii:5.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To RICHMOND]\nAwake, and think our wrongs in Richard's bosom\nWill conquer him! awake, and win the day!","sourceLine":"5.3.157","speaker":"All","id":"richardiii:5.3:67"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:5.3:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nBloody and guilty, guiltily awake,\nAnd in a bloody battle end thy days!\nThink on Lord Hastings: despair, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.160","speaker":"Ghost of HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:5.3:69"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Quiet untroubled soul, awake, awake!\nArm, fight, and conquer, for fair England's sake!","sourceLine":"5.3.164","speaker":"Ghost of HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:5.3:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghosts of the two young Princes","id":"richardiii:5.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ghosts","sourceLine":"5.3.166","speaker":"Ghost of HASTINGS","id":"richardiii:5.3:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nDream on thy cousins smother'd in the Tower:\nLet us be led within thy bosom, Richard,\nAnd weigh thee down to ruin, shame, and death!\nThy nephews' souls bid thee despair and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.167","speaker":"of young Princes","id":"richardiii:5.3:74"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sleep, Richmond, sleep in peace, and wake in joy;\nGood angels guard thee from the boar's annoy!\nLive, and beget a happy race of kings!\nEdward's unhappy sons do bid thee flourish.","sourceLine":"5.3.172","speaker":"of young Princes","id":"richardiii:5.3:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:5.3:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nRichard, thy wife, that wretched Anne thy wife,\nThat never slept a quiet hour with thee,\nNow fills thy sleep with perturbations\nTo-morrow in the battle think on me,\nAnd fall thy edgeless sword: despair, and die!","sourceLine":"5.3.176","speaker":"Ghost of LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:5.3:78"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou quiet soul, sleep thou a quiet sleep\nDream of success and happy victory!\nThy adversary's wife doth pray for thee.","sourceLine":"5.3.182","speaker":"Ghost of LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:5.3:80"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Ghost of BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ghost","sourceLine":"5.3.185","speaker":"Ghost of LADY ANNE","id":"richardiii:5.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To KING RICHARD III]\nThe last was I that helped thee to the crown;\nThe last was I that felt thy tyranny:\nO, in the battle think on Buckingham,\nAnd die in terror of thy guiltiness!\nDream on, dream on, of bloody deeds and death:\nFainting, despair; despairing, yield thy breath!","sourceLine":"5.3.186","speaker":"of BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.3:83"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I died for hope ere I could lend thee aid:\nBut cheer thy heart, and be thou not dismay'd:\nGod and good angel fight on Richmond's side;\nAnd Richard falls in height of all his pride.","sourceLine":"5.3.193","speaker":"of BUCKINGHAM","id":"richardiii:5.3:85"},{"kind":"stage","text":"The Ghosts vanish","id":"richardiii:5.3:86"},{"kind":"stage","text":"KING RICHARD III starts out of his dream","id":"richardiii:5.3:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me another horse: bind up my wounds.\nHave mercy, Jesu!--Soft! I did but dream.\nO coward conscience, how dost thou afflict me!\nThe lights burn blue. It is now dead midnight.\nCold fearful drops stand on my trembling flesh.\nWhat do I fear? myself? there's none else by:\nRichard loves Richard; that is, I am I.\nIs there a murderer here? No. Yes, I am:\nThen fly. What, from myself? Great reason why:\nLest I revenge. What, myself upon myself?\nAlack. I love myself. Wherefore? for any good\nThat I myself have done unto myself?\nO, no! alas, I rather hate myself\nFor hateful deeds committed by myself!\nI am a villain: yet I lie. I am not.\nFool, of thyself speak well: fool, do not flatter.\nMy conscience hath a thousand several tongues,\nAnd every tongue brings in a several tale,\nAnd every tale condemns me for a villain.\nPerjury, perjury, in the high'st degree\nMurder, stem murder, in the direst degree;\nAll several sins, all used in each degree,\nThrong to the bar, crying all, Guilty! guilty!\nI shall despair. There is no creature loves me;\nAnd if I die, no soul shall pity me:\nNay, wherefore should they, since that I myself\nFind in myself no pity to myself?\nMethought the souls of all that I had murder'd\nCame to my tent; and every one did threat\nTo-morrow's vengeance on the head of Richard.","sourceLine":"5.3.197","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:88"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord!","sourceLine":"5.3.227","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Zounds! who is there?","sourceLine":"5.3.228","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ratcliff, my lord; 'tis I. The early village-cock\nHath twice done salutation to the morn;\nYour friends are up, and buckle on their armour.","sourceLine":"5.3.229","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Ratcliff, I have dream'd a fearful dream!\nWhat thinkest thou, will our friends prove all true?","sourceLine":"5.3.232","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No doubt, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.234","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Ratcliff, I fear, I fear,--","sourceLine":"5.3.235","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, good my lord, be not afraid of shadows.","sourceLine":"5.3.236","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By the apostle Paul, shadows to-night\nHave struck more terror to the soul of Richard\nThan can the substance of ten thousand soldiers\nArmed in proof, and led by shallow Richmond.\nIt is not yet near day. Come, go with me;\nUnder our tents I'll play the eaves-dropper,\nTo see if any mean to shrink from me.","sourceLine":"5.3.237","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:97"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.3:98"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Lords to RICHMOND, sitting in his tent","id":"richardiii:5.3:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow, Richmond!","sourceLine":"5.3.244","speaker":"LORDS","id":"richardiii:5.3:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cry mercy, lords and watchful gentlemen,\nThat you have ta'en a tardy sluggard here.","sourceLine":"5.3.245","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How have you slept, my lord?","sourceLine":"5.3.247","speaker":"LORDS","id":"richardiii:5.3:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The sweetest sleep, and fairest-boding dreams\nThat ever enter'd in a drowsy head,\nHave I since your departure had, my lords.\nMethought their souls, whose bodies Richard murder'd,\nCame to my tent, and cried on victory:\nI promise you, my soul is very jocund\nIn the remembrance of so fair a dream.\nHow far into the morning is it, lords?","sourceLine":"5.3.248","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon the stroke of four.","sourceLine":"5.3.256","speaker":"LORDS","id":"richardiii:5.3:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then 'tis time to arm and give direction.","sourceLine":"5.3.257","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:105"},{"kind":"stage","text":"His oration to his soldiers","id":"richardiii:5.3:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"More than I have said, loving countrymen,\nThe leisure and enforcement of the time\nForbids to dwell upon: yet remember this,\nGod and our good cause fight upon our side;\nThe prayers of holy saints and wronged souls,\nLike high-rear'd bulwarks, stand before our faces;\nRichard except, those whom we fight against\nHad rather have us win than him they follow:\nFor what is he they follow? truly, gentlemen,\nA bloody tyrant and a homicide;\nOne raised in blood, and one in blood establish'd;\nOne that made means to come by what he hath,\nAnd slaughter'd those that were the means to help him;\nAbase foul stone, made precious by the foil\nOf England's chair, where he is falsely set;\nOne that hath ever been God's enemy:\nThen, if you fight against God's enemy,\nGod will in justice ward you as his soldiers;\nIf you do sweat to put a tyrant down,\nYou sleep in peace, the tyrant being slain;\nIf you do fight against your country's foes,\nYour country's fat shall pay your pains the hire;\nIf you do fight in safeguard of your wives,\nYour wives shall welcome home the conquerors;\nIf you do free your children from the sword,\nYour children's children quit it in your age.\nThen, in the name of God and all these rights,\nAdvance your standards, draw your willing swords.\nFor me, the ransom of my bold attempt\nShall be this cold corpse on the earth's cold face;\nBut if I thrive, the gain of my attempt\nThe least of you shall share his part thereof.\nSound drums and trumpets boldly and cheerfully;\nGod and Saint George! Richmond and victory!","sourceLine":"5.3.258","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.3:107"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.3:108"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter KING RICHARD, RATCLIFF, Attendants and Forces","id":"richardiii:5.3:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What said Northumberland as touching Richmond?","sourceLine":"5.3.292","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That he was never trained up in arms.","sourceLine":"5.3.293","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He said the truth: and what said Surrey then?","sourceLine":"5.3.294","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He smiled and said 'The better for our purpose.'","sourceLine":"5.3.295","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He was in the right; and so indeed it is.","sourceLine":"5.3.296","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:114"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Clock striketh","id":"richardiii:5.3:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ten the clock there. Give me a calendar.\nWho saw the sun to-day?","sourceLine":"5.3.297","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not I, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.299","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then he disdains to shine; for by the book\nHe should have braved the east an hour ago\nA black day will it be to somebody. Ratcliff!","sourceLine":"5.3.300","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"5.3.303","speaker":"RATCLIFF","id":"richardiii:5.3:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The sun will not be seen to-day;\nThe sky doth frown and lour upon our army.\nI would these dewy tears were from the ground.\nNot shine to-day! Why, what is that to me\nMore than to Richmond? for the selfsame heaven\nThat frowns on me looks sadly upon him.","sourceLine":"5.3.304","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:120"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arm, arm, my lord; the foe vaunts in the field.","sourceLine":"5.3.310","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, bustle, bustle; caparison my horse.\nCall up Lord Stanley, bid him bring his power:\nI will lead forth my soldiers to the plain,\nAnd thus my battle shall be ordered:\nMy foreward shall be drawn out all in length,\nConsisting equally of horse and foot;\nOur archers shall be placed in the midst\nJohn Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Earl of Surrey,\nShall have the leading of this foot and horse.\nThey thus directed, we will follow\nIn the main battle, whose puissance on either side\nShall be well winged with our chiefest horse.\nThis, and Saint George to boot! What think'st thou, Norfolk?","sourceLine":"5.3.311","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A good direction, warlike sovereign.\nThis found I on my tent this morning.","sourceLine":"5.3.324","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:124"},{"kind":"stage","text":"He sheweth him a paper","id":"richardiii:5.3:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads]\n'Jockey of Norfolk, be not too bold,\nFor Dickon thy master is bought and sold.'\nA thing devised by the enemy.\nGo, gentleman, every man unto his charge\nLet not our babbling dreams affright our souls:\nConscience is but a word that cowards use,\nDevised at first to keep the strong in awe:\nOur strong arms be our conscience, swords our law.\nMarch on, join bravely, let us to't pell-mell\nIf not to heaven, then hand in hand to hell.","sourceLine":"5.3.326","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:126"},{"kind":"stage","text":"His oration to his Army","id":"richardiii:5.3:127"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What shall I say more than I have inferr'd?\nRemember whom you are to cope withal;\nA sort of vagabonds, rascals, and runaways,\nA scum of Bretons, and base lackey peasants,\nWhom their o'er-cloyed country vomits forth\nTo desperate ventures and assured destruction.\nYou sleeping safe, they bring to you unrest;\nYou having lands, and blest with beauteous wives,\nThey would restrain the one, distain the other.\nAnd who doth lead them but a paltry fellow,\nLong kept in Bretagne at our mother's cost?\nA milk-sop, one that never in his life\nFelt so much cold as over shoes in snow?\nLet's whip these stragglers o'er the seas again;\nLash hence these overweening rags of France,\nThese famish'd beggars, weary of their lives;\nWho, but for dreaming on this fond exploit,\nFor want of means, poor rats, had hang'd themselves:\nIf we be conquer'd, let men conquer us,\nAnd not these bastard Bretons; whom our fathers\nHave in their own land beaten, bobb'd, and thump'd,\nAnd in record, left them the heirs of shame.\nShall these enjoy our lands? lie with our wives?\nRavish our daughters?","sourceLine":"5.3.337","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:128"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drum afar off","id":"richardiii:5.3:129"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark! I hear their drum.\nFight, gentlemen of England! fight, bold yoemen!\nDraw, archers, draw your arrows to the head!\nSpur your proud horses hard, and ride in blood;\nAmaze the welkin with your broken staves!","sourceLine":"5.3.361","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:130"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"richardiii:5.3:131"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What says Lord Stanley? will he bring his power?","sourceLine":"5.3.366","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:132"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, he doth deny to come.","sourceLine":"5.3.367","speaker":"Messenger","id":"richardiii:5.3:133"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Off with his son George's head!","sourceLine":"5.3.368","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:134"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, the enemy is past the marsh\nAfter the battle let George Stanley die.","sourceLine":"5.3.369","speaker":"NORFOLK","id":"richardiii:5.3:135"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A thousand hearts are great within my bosom:\nAdvance our standards, set upon our foes\nOur ancient word of courage, fair Saint George,\nInspire us with the spleen of fiery dragons!\nUpon them! victory sits on our helms.","sourceLine":"5.3.371","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.3:136"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.3:137"}],"scene":3},{"id":"5.4","label":"Act 5 · SCENE IV. Another part of the field.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum: excursions. Enter NORFOLK and forces fighting; to him CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rescue, my Lord of Norfolk, rescue, rescue!\nThe king enacts more wonders than a man,\nDaring an opposite to every danger:\nHis horse is slain, and all on foot he fights,\nSeeking for Richmond in the throat of death.\nRescue, fair lord, or else the day is lost!","sourceLine":"5.4.1","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.4:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarums. Enter KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A horse! a horse! my kingdom for a horse!","sourceLine":"5.4.7","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Withdraw, my lord; I'll help you to a horse.","sourceLine":"5.4.8","speaker":"CATESBY","id":"richardiii:5.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Slave, I have set my life upon a cast,\nAnd I will stand the hazard of the die:\nI think there be six Richmonds in the field;\nFive have I slain to-day instead of him.\nA horse! a horse! my kingdom for a horse!","sourceLine":"5.4.9","speaker":"KING RICHARD III","id":"richardiii:5.4:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.4:7"}],"scene":4},{"id":"5.5","label":"Act 5 · SCENE V. Another part of the field.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/richardiii/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Enter KING RICHARD III and RICHMOND; they fight. KING RICHARD III is slain. Retreat and flourish. Re-enter RICHMOND, DERBY bearing the crown, with divers other Lords","id":"richardiii:5.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God and your arms be praised, victorious friends,\nThe day is ours, the bloody dog is dead.","sourceLine":"5.5.1","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Courageous Richmond, well hast thou acquit thee.\nLo, here, this long-usurped royalty\nFrom the dead temples of this bloody wretch\nHave I pluck'd off, to grace thy brows withal:\nWear it, enjoy it, and make much of it.","sourceLine":"5.5.3","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:5.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great God of heaven, say Amen to all!\nBut, tell me, is young George Stanley living?","sourceLine":"5.5.8","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is, my lord, and safe in Leicester town;\nWhither, if it please you, we may now withdraw us.","sourceLine":"5.5.10","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:5.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What men of name are slain on either side?","sourceLine":"5.5.12","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"John Duke of Norfolk, Walter Lord Ferrers,\nSir Robert Brakenbury, and Sir William Brandon.","sourceLine":"5.5.13","speaker":"DERBY","id":"richardiii:5.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Inter their bodies as becomes their births:\nProclaim a pardon to the soldiers fled\nThat in submission will return to us:\nAnd then, as we have ta'en the sacrament,\nWe will unite the white rose and the red:\nSmile heaven upon this fair conjunction,\nThat long have frown'd upon their enmity!\nWhat traitor hears me, and says not amen?\nEngland hath long been mad, and scarr'd herself;\nThe brother blindly shed the brother's blood,\nThe father rashly slaughter'd his own son,\nThe son, compell'd, been butcher to the sire:\nAll this divided York and Lancaster,\nDivided in their dire division,\nO, now, let Richmond and Elizabeth,\nThe true succeeders of each royal house,\nBy God's fair ordinance conjoin together!\nAnd let their heirs, God, if thy will be so.\nEnrich the time to come with smooth-faced peace,\nWith smiling plenty and fair prosperous days!\nAbate the edge of traitors, gracious Lord,\nThat would reduce these bloody days again,\nAnd make poor England weep in streams of blood!\nLet them not live to taste this land's increase\nThat would with treason wound this fair land's peace!\nNow civil wounds are stopp'd, peace lives again:\nThat she may long live here, God say amen!","sourceLine":"5.5.15","speaker":"RICHMOND","id":"richardiii:5.5:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"richardiii:5.5:9"}],"scene":5}],"sourceLineCount":3700,"sourceLabel":"MIT Shakespeare","sectionCount":25,"blockCount":1368,"wordCount":29267};
